-- Publish reviewed B2 Reading questions and keys without changing answer IDs.
DO $release$
DECLARE
  item jsonb;
  answer jsonb;
  question_id uuid;
  stem_hash text;
  answer_hash text;
  changed integer;
BEGIN
  FOR item IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements($payload$[["3439ed52-bc43-4d31-8d9d-0f67c2cfb799","9b9909dbbecd318d6b63f6311fe2b0ee","7871a850ec3b585385386c12181cd99e","c4ef95c49d01067b1e7b819dc99238a2","2d4564575e64dd7c9c39e8a3e664bfb0","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nThe volunteer job I chose for the wrong reason\nI signed up to volunteer at the local community repair workshop for what I now admit was a distinctly selfish reason: I was certain that adding some volunteer work to my CV would impress future employers and help me stand out in job applications. At the time, I imagined spending my weekends fixing gadgets or tinkering with small machines — something hands-on that would demonstrate practical skills. I was convinced that volunteering was just another bullet point, a way to tick a box rather than an experience that would change how I thought about work.\nOn my first shift, however, I was surprised to find that my main task was not repairing anything but welcoming visitors and working out what help they needed. It quickly became clear that I was expected to be a friendly face, to listen to people’s requests, and then direct them to volunteers more skilled than myself. This was unexpected and initially rather frustrating. I had pictured myself elbow-deep in tools, not chatting with people or scribbling notes. But as the day went on, I realised that this role, though unglamorous, was essential for the workshop to function smoothly and for visitors to feel comfortable asking for help.\nOne particular encounter has stayed with me. A visitor came in, clearly embarrassed, clutching a small radio that wouldn’t switch on. He was sheepish about not understanding even such a simple fix, and I could tell he worried that asking for help would make him seem silly. I learned then that the way I spoke to him mattered — explaining the problem without assuming any prior knowledge, avoiding jargon, and most importantly, not making him feel foolish for not knowing how to fix something so ordinary.\nAs weeks turned into months, I took on more responsibility, gradually moving from greeting guests to coordinating the volunteers and the repair tasks depending on their skills and availability. I found myself enjoying this role far more than I expected. It gave me a sense of accomplishment and control, and I liked the social interaction. This was a side of volunteering I had not anticipated or considered when I first signed up. I discovered I was more interested in enabling others to do their work than in doing the hands-on repairs myself.\nWhen it came time to update my CV, I reflected on my experience and realised that the most valuable aspect was not the line about volunteering but what the experience taught me about myself. I now see that coordinating people and communicating effectively suit me better than the solitary, technical tasks I thought I wanted. The CV still includes a note about my volunteering, but the real benefit is a changed understanding of the kind of work I find fulfilling. Volunteering was not just about adding a badge to my applications but about discovering what I actually enjoy doing.\nLooking back, I can also laugh at some minor mistakes I made on my first day, when I proudly tried to hand a screwdriver to someone who didn’t actually want it, highlighting how clueless I was about the workshop’s unofficial routines. My previous belief that \"serious\" career skills came only from formal jobs or internships now seems limited. Doing regular, sometimes mundane tasks with real people revealed strengths I hadn’t expected in myself.\nIn essence, this volunteer role became a mirror rather than just a step on the career ladder. It reflected back to me not only what I could do but what kind of work energises me and feels worthwhile. This shift in perspective — from seeing volunteering as a strategic CV move to a personal discovery — has influenced how I think about future jobs and even how I define success. Sometimes, it’s the small, everyday interactions and responsibilities that open our eyes to new directions.\n\nQuestions\n31. How does the narrator now view their original reason for joining the workshop?\nA. They acknowledge that it was mainly about improving their own prospects.\nB. They regard it as an early sign that they wanted to work with people.\nC. They believe it showed a genuine interest in the workshop’s aims.\nD. They see it as a practical decision they would make again for the same reason.\n32. What was the narrator’s main task on the first day of volunteering? A. Repairing small machines brought in by visitors. B. Organising the volunteers and their tasks. C. Welcoming visitors and determining their needs. D. Teaching visitors how to fix items themselves.\n33. What did the encounter with the visitor and his radio teach the narrator?\nA. People are more comfortable if they can watch a repair being carried out.\nB. Visitors generally want to learn enough to fix their own equipment.\nC. A simple repair should be explained before a visitor describes the problem.\nD. The way help is offered can affect whether someone feels embarrassed about needing it.\n34. Why does the narrator mention the small mistake on their first day? A. To highlight the importance of technical skills. B. To add light humour and show initial awkwardness. C. To criticise the workshop’s informal routines. D. To explain why they stopped volunteering.\n35. What does the phrase \"a mirror rather than just a step on the career ladder\" mean in the last paragraph? A. The volunteering reflected the narrator's true interests and abilities. B. The volunteer work was only career-focused, not personal. C. The experience was disappointing and didn’t lead anywhere. D. The workshop offered a clear path to promotion and success.\n36. Which change in the narrator’s priorities is shown across the article?\nA. From wanting to organise people to wanting to repair things independently.\nB. From wanting to learn practical skills to focusing mainly on how others viewed their achievements.\nC. From wanting a different career to valuing the same work they had originally planned to do.\nD. From wanting to improve a CV to looking for work that feels personally rewarding.",[["31 A) They admitted their motive was somewhat selfish.","31 A) They acknowledge that it was mainly about improving their own prospects.",true],["31 B) They felt proud of making a strategic career decision.","31 B) They regard it as an early sign that they wanted to work with people.",false],["31 C) They believed the motive was entirely genuine.","31 C) They believe it showed a genuine interest in the workshop’s aims.",false],["31 D) They considered the motive to be irrelevant to the experience.","31 D) They see it as a practical decision they would make again for the same reason.",false],["33 A) Confident because he understood the repair process.","33 A) People are more comfortable if they can watch a repair being carried out.",false],["33 B) Frustrated at the workshop’s slow service.","33 B) Visitors generally want to learn enough to fix their own equipment.",false],["33 C) Unconcerned about the simplicity of the repair.","33 C) A simple repair should be explained before a visitor describes the problem.",false],["33 D) Embarrassed, and afraid that needing help would reflect badly on him.","33 D) The way help is offered can affect whether someone feels embarrassed about needing it.",true],["36 A) How volunteering can help develop practical repair skills.","36 A) From wanting to organise people to wanting to repair things independently.",false],["36 B) Strategies for improving job applications with volunteer experience.","36 B) From wanting to learn practical skills to focusing mainly on how others viewed their achievements.",false],["36 C) The unexpected personal insights gained through volunteering.","36 C) From wanting a different career to valuing the same work they had originally planned to do.",false],["36 D) The importance of technical knowledge in community workshops.","36 D) From wanting to improve a CV to looking for work that feels personally rewarding.",true]]],["9c9f168d-7522-42d8-80a3-f8c234529c83","c5e390a3aa3d5b1b7c59ecb788e31627","9a36bb8b8bbf29ab42634f96795ab729","547e1f10f6d86ff5b451c8a3123ef5c9","547e1f10f6d86ff5b451c8a3123ef5c9","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nThe boxes I thought I needed\nMoving into a smaller flat was supposed to be a straightforward step: fewer rooms to clean, lower bills and, I hoped, a fresh start. Instead, I spent my first evening sitting on the bare floor, hemmed in by towers of cardboard that had already claimed every corner of the sitting room. (37) Opening them only made matters worse, because most held nothing more than odds and ends I had packed out of habit rather than need: three identical chargers, a bag of assorted keys, a folder of instruction manuals for appliances I no longer owned. Somewhere between the third and fourth box, I accepted that keeping everything was not merely impractical but physically impossible.\nOne box defeated me completely. Inside, wrapped in a supermarket bag as though it were valuable, was a compact umbrella I had been given years earlier and never once opened. (38) Holding it in the middle of that half-empty room, I understood that a surprising number of my possessions were being stored for a future I had never actually described to myself. In a flat of this size, that vagueness turned out to be expensive, because every undecided object was occupying space I genuinely needed.\nA few days later, my downstairs neighbour, Ruth, mentioned a swap and repair event at the community centre. (39) That distinction mattered to me more than I expected. Throwing things away had always felt like an admission of waste, and I had been postponing decisions largely to avoid that feeling; passing something on, or watching it mended, sounded almost like the opposite.\nI arrived on the Saturday with a bag of things nobody could possibly want: a chipped mug, a scarf worn thin at the ends and a desk lamp with an unfashionable shade. The lamp went first, claimed within minutes by a student who said it was exactly right for the corner of her room. (40) What I had been calling clutter, I realised, was simply a set of guesses about other people, and my guesses were poor. Value was not a property the objects carried around with them; it depended entirely on who was standing in front of them.\nNot everything was so easy to reason about. I had also brought a hand-knitted blanket of my grandmother’s, which is warm, takes up an absurd amount of shelf space and does nothing that I actually need doing. Twice I put it on the swap table, and twice I took it back before anyone could look at it properly. (41) Some things earn their place by meaning rather than use, provided you are willing to say honestly which of the two you are claiming.\nWalking home, I found myself drafting a rule. Anything I kept had to do a job or mean something specific, and I had to be able to say which of the two it was without hesitating. (42) It has not turned me into a minimalist, and the flat is certainly not empty; it is merely more honest about what it contains and why.\nThere is still one shelf I avoid, holding a handful of objects whose value I cannot yet argue either way. I have decided that this is acceptable, at least for now. A home edited down to nothing but justifications would not be a home I would want to come back to.\n\nSentences\nA It went home with me again, and I have stopped pretending that this was an oversight.\nB The community centre had put out tea and biscuits, which struck me as a generous touch for a Saturday morning.\nC Each of these boxes was labelled ‘keep’, which told me nothing useful about what was actually inside.\nD The mug and the scarf, by contrast, attracted no interest at all, even though I had privately expected them to go before the lamp.\nE Put like that, the test sounds severe, but in practice it simply forced me to finish sentences I had been leaving unfinished for years.\nF It was not a jumble sale, she explained, but a morning for mending what was broken and rehoming what was not.\nG I had kept it ‘just in case’, although I could no longer remember what that case might be.",[]],["8118b8b8-5d14-43cc-a16b-1385ef9a69b7","e4ad3ac506de4100538a9f9afc3d18aa","a218d2ab4ed1ee6fcc938b40a3c50cdd","3ae4606b5409fb3f15df3fb00003ad9a","3ae4606b5409fb3f15df3fb00003ad9a","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…?\n43 Who began their work because they discovered stories were at risk of disappearing completely?\n44 Who talks about needing to find a compromise between keeping things original and adapting for modern requirements?\n45 Who regularly involves others in helping to identify unclear historical information?\n46 Who emphasises tailoring their approach during activities to maintain the group’s interest?\n47 Who has found that emotional memories can interfere with an accurate record of events?\n48 Who says that the work took longer and involved more routine repairs than they had expected?\n49 Who says that everyday stories can be at least as valuable as major historical events?\n50 Who feels most pleased when various pieces of information come together to solve a historical puzzle?\n51 Who avoids sharing stories that cannot be fully supported by evidence to keep trust with their audience?\n52 Who particularly enjoys doing physical repair work alongside the rest of the team?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Maya, oral-history volunteer\nInitially, I got involved after listening to an elderly neighbour sharing stories from decades ago and realising that countless personal memories were never documented. My aim has always been to preserve these individual experiences before they fade away completely. I conduct interviews with local residents, focusing on open-ended questions that encourage detailed recollections. However, it can be challenging because many people drift away from exact dates and facts, blending memories with feelings. Since then, I have learned to carefully cross-check basic facts to distinguish between what actually happened and what might be remembered inaccurately. I now value everyday stories at least as much as major historical events, but I try to remain patient and cautious to avoid mixing emotion with verified information.\n________________________________________\nB – Tom, cinema restoration volunteer\nI joined the project after noticing the old community cinema closed down and left to deteriorate, which felt like losing a shared cultural space. My motivation was to protect this building, which holds meaning for many locals. Getting my hands dirty cleaning and fixing parts of the place has been rewarding, especially working alongside others in the team. Yet, the restoration has taken much longer than I expected and is less exciting than I hoped — a lot of the work involves tedious repairs. I've also had to accept that some modern adaptations are necessary for it to be usable again, even if they affect the original look. Despite that, I’m now realistic about balancing preservation with practical use, and I’m happy the cinema might reopen in a different, functioning form.\n________________________________________\nC – Luca, digital photo archive creator\nMy interest began when I came across several boxes of old photos from families and streets around town, but most lacked labels or dates. It seemed important to organise and digitise these images to make them more accessible and searchable for anyone interested. To solve the problem of many photos being unidentified, I invite neighbours to help me tag the people and places they recognise. Still, it’s often unclear who or what is in some pictures, so I make sure to mark uncertain information clearly rather than guessing. I enjoy the process of combining small hints from various contributors to solve these historical puzzles. Currently, I feel most satisfaction when different pieces come together to reveal a photo’s story, knowing that I’ve built something collaborative and methodical.\n________________________________________\nD – Olivia, local walking-tour organiser\nFriends frequently asked me about the unusual buildings visible on everyday walks, which made me realise people often overlook the history around them. I decided to create guided walks to highlight these places, hoping to encourage locals to observe their neighbourhood more closely. Designing tours means organising routes based on visible features while keeping groups interested throughout the tour by varying pace and length. A big challenge is avoiding presenting unverified stories as facts, so I deliberately leave out legends or uncertain anecdotes. I believe it’s better to nurture curiosity while maintaining trust. Nowadays, I focus heavily on providing accurate information in a lively way, adjusting my delivery to suit various audiences, and I find the experience rewarding and energising.\n________________________________________",[]],["4e170661-2c2b-4b99-9b01-07bec6195f9f","0d6c2c90df307b9c32b50c6d28c8d8de","a31b21a5c9c41176dc01812cce5b053e","0a316e0d3958b1d9faca2e674db2c546","9809e873f1a14136abf161c524ce533c","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nWhat happens when rooftops become shared spaces\nOn the top of an unassuming apartment building in an unnamed city, a transformation is quietly taking place. Where once there was nothing but empty concrete, residents now tend a modest garden filled with vegetables, flowers and herbs. What started as a simple idea to make better use of unused rooftop areas has become a small but lively community project. This modest change raises interesting questions about shared city spaces: How do residents balance different desires for the same area? And what solutions help such projects thrive?\nThe rooftop garden at Maple View Apartments is an example of this trend. Initially inspired by city planners who promoted the idea of urban greening, the project was launched by a small group of volunteers who wanted a peaceful spot for gardening. From the outset, however, resident expectations diverged considerably. Some valued the rooftop as a quiet retreat where they could grow plants and escape the noise of the city. Others, especially younger residents, saw the space as an opportunity for social gatherings and occasional events. This difference created early tensions, as peaceful gardeners were frustrated by noise and disruption.\nBeyond these differing uses, practical matters emerged. Responsibility for watering the plants became a sticking point, especially during summer holidays when many volunteers were away. The absence of clear arrangements sometimes left plants neglected and the garden looking unkempt. In addition, the rooftop access rules, which initially allowed open entry during daylight hours, were questioned as the possibility of late-night parties and trespassing arose. The volunteers recognised these challenges needed addressing to maintain the space's appeal and usefulness.\nAfter several months, the project leaders introduced a new system of scheduled access times and a rota for watering duties. This adjustment aimed to respect the quiet atmosphere preferred by gardeners while allowing social events at agreed times. A shed was installed to store tools and watering equipment, so they would always be available to those responsible. These changes helped restore balance and allowed residents to share the rooftop fairly, reducing frustrations and misunderstandings. The practical steps taken were as important as the ideas behind the garden’s creation.\nInterestingly, not all residents engaged with the rooftop at first. Take Mr Hall, for example, who initially saw the project as a distraction with little personal benefit. However, after spending some time on the rooftop to clear his head during a stressful period, he came to appreciate the calm and fresh air it offered. Though he doesn’t participate in gardening or social events, he now values having the space as a resource for peace and relaxation. This shift shows how shared spaces can provide unexpected benefits beyond their original purpose.\nOf course, not every apartment building or community group will want or be able to manage shared rooftop spaces in the same way. The success of such initiatives depends heavily on residents’ agreement on rules and responsibilities, as well as on them adjusting those agreements in response to real-life problems. In some cases, the idea of a communal garden may be less attractive than other uses—or simply unrealistic, given the number of people involved or building design constraints.\nIn conclusion, while the environmental value of green rooftops is often highlighted, the real success lies in practical social arrangements. The routines for sharing, caring and using these spaces matter more than the initial concept. It is the everyday decisions around access, maintenance and communal respect that enable such rooftop projects to become meaningful parts of urban living.\n\nQuestions\n31. What does the opening paragraph establish about the rooftop project?\nA. The garden had already solved most of the practical problems in the building.\nB. The first aim was to make better use of an area that had been left empty.\nC. The residents had agreed how to divide the space before work began.\nD. The environmental results were the main reason the residents supported the garden.\n32. What conflicting views did the residents have about the rooftop garden? A. Some wanted a social space, others wanted quiet gardening. B. Some preferred a vegetable garden, others a flower garden. C. Some wanted to close the rooftop, others to keep it public. D. Some wished for a gym area, others for a barbecue zone.\n33. Why did the volunteers introduce a rota and scheduled access times?\nA. They needed to recruit extra gardeners to increase the size of the project.\nB. They had to make maintenance more reliable and manage competing uses of the space.\nC. They wanted to give the original gardeners priority whenever the roof was open.\nD. They hoped to solve a problem with the way the roof itself had been built.\n34. What first led Mr Hall to spend time on the roof?\nA. He was looking for somewhere to clear his head.\nB. He wanted to help with the watering while others were away.\nC. He went to see whether the rules had made events quieter.\nD. He had been invited to join a gardening activity.\n35. Why does the writer include Mr Hall’s experience?\nA. To show how objections to a project can be resolved through scheduled access.\nB. To show that a shared space can benefit people who do not take part in its planned activities.\nC. To illustrate why residents should be given a say in how a garden is maintained.\nD. To explain how the organisers found out which activities residents preferred.\n36. What can be inferred about the wider conclusion regarding rooftop projects? A. Their success depends mainly on environmental impact measurements. B. They will only work if all residents participate equally. C. Practical management and resident agreement are crucial for success. D. Such projects are uniformly suitable for all apartment buildings.",[["31 A) The environmental benefits of rooftop gardens in cities.","31 A) The garden had already solved most of the practical problems in the building.",false],["31 B) How shared rooftop spaces affect residents’ daily lives.","31 B) The first aim was to make better use of an area that had been left empty.",true],["31 C) The financial costs of transforming rooftops into gardens.","31 C) The residents had agreed how to divide the space before work began.",false],["31 D) The architectural challenges of rooftop renovations.","31 D) The environmental results were the main reason the residents supported the garden.",false],["33 A) Because the garden was not popular with most residents.","33 A) They needed to recruit extra gardeners to increase the size of the project.",false],["33 B) Due to problems caused by unclear responsibilities and misuse.","33 B) They had to make maintenance more reliable and manage competing uses of the space.",true],["33 C) Because they wanted to restrict access to certain residents.","33 C) They wanted to give the original gardeners priority whenever the roof was open.",false],["33 D) Due to complaints about the quality of the plants.","33 D) They hoped to solve a problem with the way the roof itself had been built.",false],["34 A) He initially disliked the project but later appreciated its calm atmosphere.","34 A) He was looking for somewhere to clear his head.",true],["34 B) He was always enthusiastic and constantly used the garden for social events.","34 B) He wanted to help with the watering while others were away.",false],["34 C) He refused to accept the rooftop changes and avoided the space entirely.","34 C) He went to see whether the rules had made events quieter.",false],["34 D) He preferred the rooftop to become a parking area instead.","34 D) He had been invited to join a gardening activity.",false],["35 A) To show that rooftop spaces always attract everyone’s attention equally.","35 A) To show how objections to a project can be resolved through scheduled access.",false],["35 B) To demonstrate an unexpected benefit of shared spaces beyond gardening.","35 B) To show that a shared space can benefit people who do not take part in its planned activities.",true],["35 C) To provide evidence that some residents disapprove of rooftop gardens.","35 C) To illustrate why residents should be given a say in how a garden is maintained.",false],["35 D) To emphasise that gardening should be the only use of rooftops.","35 D) To explain how the organisers found out which activities residents preferred.",false]]],["0d45f846-6919-407d-9c3b-17a096024264","278a435d157199e84f011d10af889771","fc5decf3b5b51e935fc78cbc29531b52","ac3fa02676145000a3ea10882b9e0cb2","ac3fa02676145000a3ea10882b9e0cb2","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nThe evening routine that was really a work problem\nI always believed that the secret to feeling refreshed in the evening was a carefully planned routine. I would arrange my time after work with precision—brewing herbal tea just right, reading a chapter of a book, and avoiding screens. Yet, despite all my best efforts, those evenings still felt exhausting rather than relaxing. (37) It was clear that no combination of relaxing activities could fix this constant tiredness.\nEven after all those surface-level changes, I found myself mentally sifting through unfinished tasks, replaying email threads in my mind or wondering if I’d missed a message. I tried turning off notifications and setting strict times to check work emails, hoping that cutting back on screen time would ease my mind. (38) But the nagging feeling of not having fully “logged off” from work haunted every attempt.\nThis only became undeniable one evening when I found a reminder for an incomplete task pinned to my desk, which I hadn’t addressed properly during the day. That small interruption made me realise how much unfinished work I was carrying mentally into my personal time. I wasn’t really ending my workday at all; the boundary between work and home was simply blurred. (39) From that moment, I understood that my exhaustion was less about bedtime habits, and more about not closing the chapter on work.\nMotivated by this insight, I introduced a straightforward end-of-work ritual: I would write down the first task for the next day, organise my workspace, and physically put away all work-related materials. This simple act became my signal that work was truly over for the day and that the evening belonged to me. (40) I found comfort in this clear separation, though it took some effort to stick to it consistently.\nHowever, old habits died hard. On several occasions, I felt tempted to reopen my laptop just to check one last thing or respond to a quick message. That tiny slip would pull me back into work mode, undoing the mental boundary I had tried to set. (41) Yet, recognising the pattern helped me resist these urges more effectively each time, and I grew more confident in protecting my evening space.\nGradually, I noticed that my attention in the evenings shifted towards activities that were not tied to work. I didn’t suddenly start sleeping like a baby or claim miraculous energy boosts, but there was a difference in how occupied my mind felt. Evening time felt less dominated by work worries and more open for genuine relaxation. (42) This transition, although subtle, was enough to change my experience of after-hours completely.\nLooking back, I realise that the useful change was never about finding a perfect sleep routine. Instead, it was about establishing a clear boundary between working hours and personal time. Creating that end-of-day line allowed me to mentally switch off without any unrealistic promises about sleep quality. There’s no magic formula for sleep, but treating evenings as a space free from work chatter has certainly made a difference for me.\n\nSentences\nA I would get to the end of an evening like that already wondering what I ought to try the next day.\nB The same desk had once been used for evening classes, when finishing work on time was rarely a problem.\nC That at least meant I was no longer being interrupted by alerts as I tried to relax.\nD With tomorrow’s first task written down, I no longer had to keep rehearsing it in my head in case I forgot it.\nE The note had stayed on the desk, but the job it represented seemed to have followed me into the rest of the flat.\nF One glance at the inbox could become ten minutes spent composing a reply I had never intended to send.\nG I began picking up a book because I wanted to know what happened next, rather than because reading was on my list.",[]],["43df2881-d848-424e-801e-bdca93addb62","f722dd9b999844a637aac7760a22c16e","d490f6da003023d6bed5ecb93ddb64a2","049bb383eb0cade88ad66f59f7bbe134","049bb383eb0cade88ad66f59f7bbe134","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person found the particular change most helpful in their work? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who initially expected a new role to slow them down and found they had to rethink procedures they took for granted?\n44 Who appreciates time periods set aside to protect uninterrupted working?\n45 Who values the freedom to decide when to start and finish work within an agreed timeframe?\n46 Who finds that their change requires careful communication to coordinate with others working differently?\n47 Who thinks that a balance between focus and social interaction is important for effective work?\n48 Who initially felt their new routine was awkward but later found ways to adapt successfully?\n49 Who values practical teamwork sessions but dislikes when discussions don’t lead to action?\n50 Who feels that having control over their working hours is more important than simply reducing hours?\n51 Who benefits from a role that lets them feel more useful by supporting a colleague?\n52 Who got into the habit of writing down questions or thoughts to deal with after a protected work period?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Alex - protected focus periods\nI work mainly on complex design and analysis tasks that require deep concentration, but constant interruptions used to make this difficult. Our team decided to introduce fixed hours when nobody could schedule meetings or send direct messages, so we could focus without distractions. Initially, it felt a bit strange to pause normal communication, and some quick questions had to wait until the blocks ended. However, I learnt to jot down thoughts and handle them later, which helped maintain my flow. While I appreciate these protected focus periods for allowing me to complete demanding work without switching tasks, I wouldn’t want them all day. For me, balancing uninterrupted time with moments to interact is key; I value clear boundaries that respect concentration but without total isolation.\n________________________________________\nB – Beth - flexible scheduling\nHaving strict start times for work was a source of daily stress, especially since I often have personal responsibilities around the same hours. When management offered the chance to choose start and finish times within a certain window, it made a huge difference. I still stick to agreed hours overall, but deciding exactly when to begin and end gives me much more control. Of course, this flexibility means we must communicate handovers carefully to avoid confusion with colleagues on different schedules. We use shared status notes for this, making sure the next person knows what’s happening. Ultimately, I find managing my own workday more important than reducing the number of hours worked. Predictability combined with choice has become my top priority.\n________________________________________\nC – Charlie - mentoring a new colleague\nWhen I was asked to mentor a new team member, my first thought was that it would add extra workload and slow me down. Mentoring requires patience because explaining what’s second nature to me means reflecting on procedures I’d never questioned before. Over time, though, I realised I was helping someone become independent, which felt rewarding beyond expectations. I also found that teaching tasks helped me see them more clearly myself and identify areas for my own improvement. Even though mentoring occasionally means repeating myself and takes time away from my own tasks, I appreciate the sense of usefulness it brings and the quiet pride in contributing to someone else’s growth.\n________________________________________\nD – Dana - problem-solving meetings\nOur team used to deal with recurring issues individually, which meant the same problems were raised repeatedly without real solutions. We introduced short, regular problem-solving sessions where one member presents an issue and everyone affected discusses possible fixes. We end each meeting by agreeing on one practical action to follow up. Initially, it was a challenge to keep these discussions efficient and avoid going over the same points. Some meetings felt repetitive when no clear decisions were made. Nonetheless, the process encouraged shared responsibility and a better understanding of challenges across the team. While I appreciate this collaborative approach, I’m impatient with meetings that don’t lead to concrete outcomes.\n________________________________________",[]],["af5524b5-eb69-483a-870f-a1d8cda67b62","21ad207223ac568004fa010aedecc193","9d177d80a7b81a8dc4b2025ef5d56294","96a03a53674a3779d8409367e3ea8844","96a03a53674a3779d8409367e3ea8844","Part 7: Multiple matching\nYou are going to read an article in which four people talk about learning a language without attending a class. For questions 43–52, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who was pushed into learning by a date that cannot be moved?\n44 Who had no choice in the matter, because the people around them at work spoke nothing else?\n45 Who admits to giving a dishonest reason for avoiding an arrangement?\n46 Who cannot tell how they sound to people outside the family because they have hardly spoken to any?\n47 Who gave up a study habit after realising it had been a way of avoiding difficulty?\n48 Who learned how to carry out tasks before understanding how the language works?\n49 Who copes with a conversation only while the number of speakers stays low?\n50 Who can talk at length about their own field but not about practical everyday arrangements?\n51 Who has come to doubt that what they have been counting reflects real ability?\n52 Who judges progress by being able to begin work without confirming they have understood?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Ruth\nI have eight months. That is not a boast, it is a deadline: the job moves in June and the family goes with it. So I meet the same person every Thursday in a café and we talk for an hour, half in each language. I prepare three subjects beforehand. I no longer write anything down during the hour, because the notebook was how I used to escape. My weakness is embarrassing. I can discuss my own work for twenty minutes and then fail completely to arrange a delivery or explain a leak to a plumber. If he corrects me in the middle of a sentence I lose the thread, and for the rest of the hour I avoid whatever caused it. A good Thursday is one where I said what I meant once and was understood.\n________________________________________\nB – Nadia\nIt began with my husband’s mother, who has never had a conversation with me that somebody else was not translating. I listen on the train, forty minutes each way, and I have got into the habit of playing the same recordings over and over until there is nothing left in them that surprises me. That has made me a strange sort of learner. Sitting at their table I can follow two people perfectly well, and the moment a third and a fourth join in I lose all of it and go back to smiling. In two years I have not said more than a sentence to anyone outside the family, so I have no idea what I sound like to a stranger. What I count now is how much of an evening I can follow without anyone stopping to explain it to me afterwards.\n________________________________________\nC – Deniz\nI took the job because it paid better. Nobody mentioned that the kitchen ran in another language and that I would be the only one who did not have it. For the first fortnight I learned the instructions and nothing else: the words for pass me that, behind you, two minutes, start again. Grammar came a long way afterwards and some of it has still not arrived. I get corrected twenty times a shift and it does not trouble me in the slightest; on a Friday night nobody has time to be polite about it. Outside the building I am useless. I have never held a conversation in it that lasted five minutes. What I go by is whether I can be handed a job now and start it without checking that I have understood.\n________________________________________\nD – Gerald\nI set myself the challenge the week after I retired, and I have completed something every single day since — seven hundred and forty-one days, which I mention only because the number is the whole difficulty. I have a large vocabulary that has never once left my head. I can recognise a word instantly and produce nothing. Twice I arranged to meet a speaker for coffee, and twice, I am sorry to say, I cancelled the day before with a reason that was not entirely true. What I have done instead is start on a newspaper column, which at least resists me. I am beginning to suspect, though I have not yet acted on it, that what I have been measuring for two years is my consistency rather than my language.\n________________________________________",[]],["1fa3f929-b385-407a-8674-94a3ea5cac1d","0b8d3731b51251a7545b3a4562bb339a","7cd94eb1aced2cd8dc911ea90e769388","e1d6664d5a457d371bb299a067b73ca5","e1d6664d5a457d371bb299a067b73ca5","Part 7: Multiple matching\nYou are going to read an article in which four people talk about letting out accommodation. For questions 43–52, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who rented out a converted building because letting it was the only way to justify the cost of bringing it back into use?\n44 Who withdrew something they used to provide because of the conversation it forced?\n45 Who found a bed for both parties when their own record of bookings proved wrong?\n46 Who believes that complaints are really about effort the guest never sees?\n47 Who restricts the days on which guests may stay in order to keep part of the week private?\n48 Who has accepted a lower score rather than supply what visitors ask for?\n49 Who keeps the places they look after indistinguishable from one another on purpose?\n50 Who used to excuse themselves for replying slowly and has given that up?\n51 Who is direct about having started purely for the money?\n52 Who tells visitors in advance what the surroundings will actually be like?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Priya\nThe rent went up by two hundred a month and I had a spare room. That is the whole story, and I am not going to dress it up as anything else. What nobody warns you about is that there is no version of this where you finish for the day. My flat is the business. So I have made rules and I keep to them. I take bookings from Monday to Thursday and the weekend is mine, whatever anybody offers me for it. I used to do breakfast and I stopped, because breakfast means talking to a stranger at seven in the morning before I have decided who I am that day. I answer messages twice, once at eight and once at six, and anyone who needs a faster reply can book elsewhere.\n________________________________________\nB – Marged\nThe building had stood empty for eleven years and the roof was going. I did the conversion because I would rather it was used than sold, and letting it was the only way to justify what that cost. Guests ask for things I am not going to provide. They want a hot tub, they want the yard lit at night, they want the cattle to be quieter than cattle are. I say no, my rating has come down for it, and I have decided I can live with that. What I do instead is write to people properly before they arrive and set out exactly what the place is: a working farm, with the hours and the mud that go with one. The ones who take it for a full week have understood by the third day.\n________________________________________\nC – Owen\nIt started as a favour for a neighbour who had bought a flat and never lived in it. There are three now, and I treat it as what it is, which is a job with a defined scope. The locks are automatic, the messages are automatic, the cleaners work to a schedule I set in January, and the pricing adjusts itself. All of that took a year to build and it runs without me. The one thing I have never handed over is the arrival. I meet every guest, I give them ten minutes, and after that they do not see me. People occasionally complain that the whole thing feels impersonal. What they are objecting to is work they cannot see. The three flats are deliberately identical, and that is not laziness, it is the point.\n________________________________________\nD – Josephine\nI have been letting rooms since long before any of this happened on a screen. The walkers came past the gate, they needed a bed, and the house has nine of them and one of me. What changed was not the guests, who are much as they always were, but the speed everyone now expects. Somebody messages at half past ten at night and is put out that I have not replied by eleven; in August I am not going to reply by eleven, and I gave up apologising for that some years ago. I still keep the diary, the paper one, and where the diary and the system disagree the diary is right. It has been wrong twice, in fairness, and on both occasions I found the second party a bed here anyway.\n________________________________________",[]],["de65d64a-5d50-4306-b9ac-4c3d7a00c68b","bbfc8fa531144dea1876d214bad5dd8f","5df3b8d4dacbfec682b8ef753c508a93","993f48b20da41a4c87bca49a73d4a751","993f48b20da41a4c87bca49a73d4a751","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nSmall Ideas, Big Changes\nWhen people talk about life-changing inventions, they often mention planes, computers or the internet. Yet many of the objects that shape our days are far less dramatic. We use them so automatically that it is easy to forget they had to be designed, tested and accepted by the public. Their importance lies not in how exciting they seem, but in how completely they have blended into ordinary routines. (37) Once that happens, daily life begins to feel different in ways that are hard to reverse.\nOne good example is the humble zip. Before it became common, buttons, hooks and laces did the same jobs, but usually more slowly and with greater effort. Early versions of the zip were not especially reliable, and some clothing companies worried that customers would not trust them. (38) As manufacturers improved the design, however, people started to appreciate the speed and convenience it offered, especially on children's clothes, bags and boots.\nAnother invention that transformed everyday life was the ballpoint pen. Cheap, light and easy to carry, it solved a problem that had frustrated writers for years: fountain pens leaked, needed careful refilling and often stopped working at inconvenient moments. The new pen was not instantly perfect, and some early models were disappointing. (39) By the time production methods improved, though, the ballpoint had become practical enough for offices, schools and homes.\nThe electric washing machine had an even deeper effect because it changed not just a single task but the rhythm of domestic life. Washing clothes by hand was tiring, time-consuming work, and in many homes it occupied a full day every week. (40) This mattered especially in larger families, where the amount of washing could be overwhelming. What had once required constant physical effort gradually became a more manageable background task.\nNot every useful invention is mechanical. Supermarkets, for instance, depended on a social invention as much as a technical one: the barcode. Shops had always needed a way to identify products and calculate prices, but doing this by hand was slow and often inaccurate. (41) It also made stock control easier, helping shops know what needed replacing before shelves became empty.\nPerhaps the most powerful inventions are the ones that remove tiny obstacles again and again. The sticky note is a good case in point. It was created from an adhesive that was weaker than expected, which might normally have been seen as a failure. Instead, someone realised that a note which could be attached and removed without damage would be useful in offices, classrooms and kitchens. (42) Because they are so simple, we rarely notice how often they save us time.\nInventions like these rarely appear in history books beside grand scientific breakthroughs. Even so, they deserve attention because they show that progress is not always about doing spectacular new things. Sometimes it is about making familiar tasks easier, quicker and slightly less annoying. That quieter kind of innovation may be less impressive at first sight, but it is often what changes everyday life most completely.\n\nSentences\nA The barcode let a scanner identify the product and find its price without either step being done by hand.\nB In fact, many successful inventions become almost invisible precisely because they work so well.\nC For that reason, the first users were not always convinced that it was better than older alternatives.\nD Once a machine could do the washing, that day was no longer tied to a single household task.\nE These removable notes put a reminder exactly where it was needed, without making it permanent.\nF In some cases, inventors deliberately kept the original materials unchanged in order to protect traditional manufacturing methods.\nG This hesitation meant that the device first appeared on items where practicality mattered more than style.",[]],["e3490f04-2c9e-42ef-b450-418d46ebdeda","2fe69f2c3e9a84c009059730ae738304","8c2ca4bef77f477e0a1b66d1d105ce9f","ff5f380da76b88e25141a804a5c875e2","ff5f380da76b88e25141a804a5c875e2","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who had to deal with a problem left behind by the site's previous use?\n44 Who reports that residents themselves said the scheme had made their lives better?\n45 Who found that people outside the project doubted it would work?\n46 Who has since taken on the role of pushing for similar schemes elsewhere?\n47 Who believes official financial support is currently insufficient?\n48 Who talks about an increase in environmental awareness among peers?\n49 Who describes people using the new space to relax during the working day?\n50 Who found it difficult at first to organise the people who had volunteered?\n51 Who reports that the scheme was welcomed in spite of objections from one group?\n52 Who believes that residents are more likely to care for a space when they feel it belongs to them?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Sophie\nWhen my city council proposed to transform an old industrial site into a community garden, I was excited. Initially, I was sceptical about the project's feasibility, given the site's historical contamination. However, after thorough soil treatments and planning, we successfully created a vibrant space. It took teamwork and dedication, but seeing local residents coming together to enjoy the green urban oasis is rewarding. The positive effects on our mental health and community spirit are undeniable. Since then, I've been advocating for additional green projects to enhance urban living. Although some challenges remain, the benefits of having green spaces close to home make it worthwhile. We've also noticed how the garden has made the area feel cleaner and more pleasant. Despite some opposition, particularly from motorists who lost parking spaces, overall, the initiative has been embraced warmly.\n________________________________________\nB – Liam\nA few years ago, I initiated a rooftop garden project on top of the commercial building where I work. The primary goal was to reduce the building's energy use and carbon footprint by improving insulation. Initially, raising awareness and funding were challenging, but persistence paid off. The garden now boasts a range of plants that not only beautify the space but also benefit the environment. Colleagues have embraced the opportunity to unwind in the tranquil setting during breaks. Furthermore, it's sparked a noticeable shift in attitudes towards environmental responsibility among staff. There's now a growing interest in expanding similar projects throughout the city. Despite lacking expertise initially, I've learned a lot about sustainable practices. The transformation has been remarkable, and I'm proud to have led this initiative, proving that even small changes can lead to significant impacts.\n________________________________________\nC – Emily\nAs a city planner, I've always prioritised integrating nature within urban developments. My latest project faced numerous obstacles, including budget constraints and scepticism from the public. However, the urban park's grand opening marked a turning point. The park, equipped with walking trails and picnic areas, aims to provide residents with a retreat from the bustling city life. Seeing families enjoying the new facilities validated the relentless effort my team invested. Community feedback has been overwhelmingly positive, highlighting the park's role in enhancing quality of life. However, there are ongoing discussions about maintaining the park sustainably. Addressing these challenges is critical, as I believe urban green spaces are essential for future developments. The park's success has encouraged further investment in green initiatives, showing people the tangible benefits of environmental stewardship.\n________________________________________\nD – Oliver\nMy experience with green urban projects began when I joined a volunteering initiative to revitalise neglected neighbourhood parks. It quickly became evident that many people desire spaces that foster community interaction and add beauty to urban life. At first, coordinating volunteers was tough, but witnessing the transformation of these neglected areas made it worthwhile. There's a unique joy in seeing children play and families gather in spaces they once avoided. Over time, my enthusiasm for green spaces only grew. These projects have taught me the significance of integrating nature into urban settings to combat stress and promote well-being. Although I'd like to see more government funding, community-driven efforts have shown potential to bring about change. The sense of ownership that residents feel towards these spaces contributes significantly to their upkeep and preservation.\n________________________________________",[]],["df0c68ba-ad5a-4f4d-9487-1cb04c91bda9","69ebd135210e79129381f85e925b71cf","05c7a9f953643a81129bd9ebc1c3d7cd","bf6780a18ae73bf2e3d87ffdb857cd4f","bf6780a18ae73bf2e3d87ffdb857cd4f","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nWorking With the Machine\nA few years ago, stories about artificial intelligence at work usually focused on robots replacing people. The reality in many offices has turned out to be less dramatic, but in some ways more surprising. AI systems are now booking meetings, summarising reports and sorting customer emails before a human even sees them. (37) As a result, discussions about the future of work have become more practical, with managers and employees asking what should be automated and what should remain firmly in human hands.\nOne reason for this change is that the latest tools are easy to access. In the past, only large firms with specialist teams could afford to experiment with advanced software. Now even small businesses can pay for online services that write advertising copy, analyse sales figures or help staff organise their day. (38) Instead, they are often built into familiar workplace platforms, which means people start using them almost without noticing.\nThat quiet arrival can bring clear advantages. In hospitals, for example, AI can organise patient information so doctors spend less time on paperwork. In law firms, it can search huge collections of documents far faster than junior staff. (39) When routine tasks disappear, workers often have more time for decisions that require judgement, experience and direct contact with other people.\nHowever, the same technology also creates new pressure. If a machine can produce a first draft of a report in seconds, employers may expect staff to complete more work in less time. Some workers say they now spend part of the day checking AI-generated material rather than producing their own. (40) In other words, efficiency does not always feel like freedom from the employee's point of view.\nThere is also the question of trust. AI tools are impressive, but they still make mistakes, and those mistakes can be hard to spot because the language they produce sounds confident. A chatbot may invent a source, while an image system might create misleading visual details. (41) For that reason, companies are increasingly training staff not just to use AI, but to question it carefully.\nAnother issue is what happens to workplace skills over time. If software always writes the first version of an email or presentation, younger employees may have fewer chances to develop those abilities themselves. Some experts worry this could weaken the very talents businesses will continue to need. (42) The challenge, then, is not simply to save time today, but to make sure tomorrow's workforce still knows how to think, write and solve problems independently.\nFor now, most evidence suggests that AI is changing jobs more often than it is eliminating them. New roles are appearing for people who can train systems, review outputs and decide when automation is useful. At the same time, older roles are being reshaped rather than wiped out completely. The workplace of the future may include more machines, but success will still depend on people knowing when to rely on them and when to switch them off.\n\nSentences\nA Yet this shift has not removed work so much as altered the kind of work people do.\nB Even so, many companies have discovered that using AI effectively still depends on human oversight.\nC These tools do not usually arrive as dramatic new machines in the corner of the room.\nD This can mean the pace increases even when the task itself seems easier.\nE The trouble is that a plausible answer may be hardest to question when the reader knows least about the subject.\nF In creative industries, meanwhile, it can generate ideas quickly enough to help teams get started.\nG Some businesses have responded by banning personal phones during the working day.",[]],["c66dbe6f-ba6b-4850-b1e5-6d1969695e2a","f3b52eefe7eac43c3b710cc89dad8f2f","04d85cac717befc3b6b5aa2a61938d5a","966258542e3a12e8d5c1955bc7dcecde","966258542e3a12e8d5c1955bc7dcecde","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who found that being on their own changed the way others reacted to them?\n44 Who realised that having complete freedom could create problems as well as advantages?\n45 Who values being able to spend a whole day exploring one subject that most of their friends would find uninteresting?\n46 Who began travelling independently as a result of another person's change of mind?\n47 Who began reserving the first few nights of a trip after an over-flexible itinerary caused problems?\n48 Who admits they briefly wanted to end a trip early when they were not enjoying part of it?\n49 Who values the chance to observe places carefully rather than to socialise?\n50 Who began travelling alone partly to escape from the pressures of normal life?\n51 Who points out that solo travel was a practical solution to having different tastes from someone close to them?\n52 Who suggests that being by yourself can make ordinary travel decisions feel more difficult?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Leonie Marsh\nI started travelling alone almost by accident after a friend pulled out of a walking holiday in Portugal. Initially, I was annoyed rather than excited, but once I arrived, I realised how much easier it was to follow my own rhythm. If I wanted to spend three hours sketching in a fishing village, nobody minded. That freedom soon became the main attraction. However, I did discover that solo travel demands more practical effort than going away with company. When a train was cancelled, for instance, there was no one to compare ideas with, so every decision felt heavier. Since then, I have become far more organised before setting off. I still enjoy chatting to strangers in cafés and hostels, yet I do not travel alone in order to meet people. For me, the real benefit is having enough quiet time to notice places properly.\n________________________________________\nB – Rafiq Siddons\nFor years I avoided solo trips because I assumed they would feel lonely. Meanwhile, most of my friends were posting photographs from group holidays that looked far more entertaining. Then work sent me to Helsinki for a conference, and I added a few extra days on my own. To my surprise, being alone made me more approachable. In a group, I tend to stay with the people I know, whereas this time I ended up sharing meals with locals and other visitors. Not every moment was comfortable: one wet Sunday afternoon, I seriously considered booking an earlier flight home because the city seemed to empty out. Yet that brief low point taught me something useful. If you keep moving, even just to a gallery or market, the mood usually changes. I now plan at least one solo break each year, mainly because it pushes me beyond familiar habits.\n________________________________________\nC – Marta Villiers\nMy first independent journey came after a difficult year when I needed distance from everyday routines. I chose a rail pass and spent two weeks crossing central Europe with no detailed schedule. Friends described this as brave, but I saw it differently: travelling alone meant I did not have to negotiate every stop or justify changing my mind. Even so, the complete lack of structure was not as relaxing as I had imagined. After missing a connection in Prague, I found myself wishing I had booked at least the first few nights in advance. Since then, I have kept the spirit of flexibility while building in a framework. I reserve accommodation for the start of a trip, then leave room for last-minute choices later on. That balance suits me. I still return home refreshed, but I no longer confuse spontaneity with good planning.\n________________________________________\nD – Noah Kerr\nUnlike some solo travellers, I did not set off in search of freedom or self-discovery. My reason was simpler: my partner hates long train journeys and museums, both of which I love. Rather than arguing over holiday plans, we sometimes travel separately for short periods. At first, I worried that people would assume I was alone because something had gone wrong in my life. In reality, the experience felt perfectly ordinary once I got used to eating dinner by myself. What I value most is the chance to become deeply absorbed in one subject. On a recent trip to northern Italy, I spent an entire day comparing small railway museums, which would have bored most of my friends. That said, I am not interested in proving I can manage everything independently. If a local guide offers expertise, I am happy to join a tour and learn more.\n________________________________________",[]],["8cbdd5e1-d83b-4cf0-89da-d2a445ed97a2","0d37fe15ea6900ec5bfacd318695bb71","0709b14cab780bf2d9874947316ff219","d10353709def9ead80414c8299ad3f87","bd3dd1a39a352ddb4f1d742aefb019f9","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nFitness That Fits Real Life\nMany people decide to get fitter in the same dramatic way: they buy expensive trainers, join a gym and promise themselves they will go five times a week. Two weeks later, the trainers are by the door, the gym card is in a drawer and the plan has quietly disappeared. This cycle is so common that it is easy to believe staying fit must involve a huge effort. In fact, the opposite is often true. The routines that last are usually the ones that fit into daily life so neatly that they barely feel like routines at all.\nOne reason people give up is that they choose exercise that looks impressive rather than exercise they will actually do. A friend may be training for a half marathon, and social media is full of intense workout videos, but that does not mean running long distances or lifting heavy weights is the right starting point for everyone. If your first plan already feels like punishment, you are unlikely to repeat it. Walking to work, cycling to the shops or doing a short online class in the living room may sound less exciting, but they are far easier to keep doing when work gets busy or the weather changes.\nThis does not mean easy exercise has little value. In recent years, health experts have become much more interested in ordinary movement spread across the day. Climbing stairs, carrying shopping bags and standing up regularly from a desk can all make a difference, especially for people who spend hours sitting down. Ten minutes here and there may not look important in isolation, yet the effect adds up. The point is not that a brisk walk is equal to a serious training session, but that people often ignore useful activity because it does not match their image of what exercise should look like.\nThere is also a psychological advantage in keeping things simple. Large fitness goals can be inspiring, but they can also create an uncomfortable feeling of failure before you have properly begun. If the target is to become a different person by next month, any missed session seems to prove you were never committed. Smaller goals work differently. Promising yourself a short walk after dinner or a few stretches before breakfast makes success more likely, and success matters because it changes identity. Once people start seeing themselves as someone who moves regularly, they often become more willing to do a little more.\nOf course, convenience should not be confused with carelessness. People sometimes use the idea of \"keeping active\" to avoid anything that raises the heart rate or requires planning. Fetching a cup of tea from the kitchen is movement, but it is not enough on its own. The most helpful approach is probably a mixture: build more activity into ordinary life, then protect a small number of moments each week for something slightly harder. That could mean a swim on Saturday, a longer bike ride or a dance class with a friend. The details matter less than the fact that the habit is realistic.\nPerhaps the most useful change, then, is not in the body but in the story people tell themselves about fitness. If exercise is treated as a test of discipline, many will delay starting until they have the right clothes, enough time or stronger motivation. If it is seen instead as a practical part of everyday life, the barrier becomes lower. You do not have to wait for a perfect Monday or a burst of enthusiasm. You can begin with the journey to work, the stairs at the station or ten minutes before bed, and let consistency do the rest.\n\nQuestions\n31. What point does the opening make about the people who buy trainers and join a gym?\nA. Buying equipment makes it easier to maintain a demanding plan.\nB. Their plans are hard to sustain when the changes fit poorly into daily life.\nC. They tend to lose interest when they cannot see a quick improvement in fitness.\nD. Their main difficulty is choosing between exercising at home and at a gym.\n32. In the second paragraph, the writer suggests that some people choose certain forms of exercise mainly because they\nA. want results as quickly as possible.\nB. have been advised to copy fitter friends.\nC. are influenced by what seems admirable or fashionable.\nD. believe easier activities are suitable only for beginners.\n33. Why does the writer mention climbing stairs, carrying shopping bags and standing up from a desk?\nA. to argue that these should replace more demanding exercise completely\nB. to show that useful physical activity can be built into ordinary routines\nC. to explain why office workers need different advice from other people\nD. to prove that short periods of exercise are better than longer ones\n34. What is the writer's attitude towards large fitness goals in the fourth paragraph?\nA. They are helpful only when supported by professional coaching.\nB. They are mostly unnecessary once people have formed good habits.\nC. They are motivating for everyone despite the pressure they create.\nD. They can be useful, but they may also discourage people at the start.\n35. In the fifth paragraph, what does 'That' refer to in the sentence 'That could mean a swim on Saturday, a longer bike ride or a dance class with a friend'?\nA. using everyday tasks as the only form of exercise\nB. avoiding activities that need any preparation\nC. setting aside time for a more demanding activity\nD. increasing the amount of movement done at work\n36. Which statement best summarises the writer's overall message?\nA. People should wait until they feel highly motivated before starting to exercise seriously.\nB. Fitness becomes easier to maintain when it is treated as a normal part of daily life.\nC. The best way to get fit is to combine social activities with strict weekly targets.\nD. Small changes are worthwhile only if they eventually lead to an ambitious training plan.",[["31 A) People often spend too much money before deciding what kind of exercise they enjoy.","31 A) Buying equipment makes it easier to maintain a demanding plan.",false],["31 B) Fitness plans usually fail because they are based on sudden, unrealistic changes.","31 B) Their plans are hard to sustain when the changes fit poorly into daily life.",true],["31 C) Gym memberships are less useful than people expect them to be.","31 C) They tend to lose interest when they cannot see a quick improvement in fitness.",false],["31 D) The hardest part of getting fit is finding time in a busy schedule.","31 D) Their main difficulty is choosing between exercising at home and at a gym.",false]]],["bc0de7a4-bd2f-4e69-bc78-d5a43250b146","6584aa33205eab0352a007d5d5b6bbe5","287dc64cbbdac743f224bc22c09d30fb","ed8d521a97bbbe7d015be409476499f7","ed8d521a97bbbe7d015be409476499f7","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nAlways Connected?\nA generation ago, staying in touch usually meant planning ahead. People arranged a time to call, wrote letters that took days to arrive, or waited until they met face to face. Today, a message can cross the world in seconds, and that speed has changed not only how often we communicate but also what we expect from one another. Friends, relatives and colleagues can now reach us almost whenever they want, wherever we happen to be.\nFor many people, the biggest change is the disappearance of distance as a real obstacle. Video calls allow grandparents to read bedtime stories from another country, while work teams can discuss a project without sharing an office. (37) As a result, people often maintain relationships that might once have become weaker over time. Regular contact no longer depends on living in the same place, or even in the same time zone.\nAnother major shift is the movement from delayed replies to instant reactions. In the past, silence usually had a practical explanation: a person was out, a letter had not arrived, or a phone line was busy. Now, when someone sees a message and does not answer, others may read meaning into that pause. (38) Technology has therefore created a new form of social pressure, in which speed itself can seem like a sign of care, interest or respect.\nAt the same time, digital communication has changed the style of everyday language. Emails were once seen as quite formal, but messaging apps have encouraged shorter sentences, quicker exchanges and a more relaxed tone. (39) Although some critics worry that this makes writing less careful, others point out that people are actually writing far more often than previous generations did. Teenagers who rarely send letters may still produce thousands of words a week on their phones.\nThe rise of group chats has brought its own advantages and problems. Families use them to share news, sports teams organise lifts there, and groups of friends make plans without sending ten separate messages. (40) Yet these spaces can also be exhausting. Conversations continue all day, jokes arrive too quickly for everyone to follow, and some members feel obliged to react even when they have nothing useful to add.\nPublic communication has changed as well. Social media allows individuals, companies and public figures to speak directly to large audiences without relying on traditional newspapers or broadcasters. (41) A funny post, a short video or a personal comment can reach millions within hours. This has made communication more democratic in some ways, but it has also made mistakes more visible and harder to control once they begin to spread.\nDespite all these developments, older forms of communication have not completely disappeared. Important conversations are still often saved for face-to-face meetings, especially when emotions are involved or decisions are difficult. (42) In the end, technology has not replaced human communication; it has expanded it. We now have more ways to connect than ever before, but choosing the right one has become a skill in itself.\n\nSentences\nA This has completely altered who gets heard and how quickly ideas move from private conversation into public debate.\nB For that reason, a handwritten note or a carefully timed phone call can still feel more meaningful than a stream of short messages.\nC Some experts believe that voice technology will soon replace keyboards for most online conversations.\nD In many cases, that can strengthen family life and make international friendships feel surprisingly ordinary.\nE A single thread may serve all these purposes for several people at once.\nF A row of laughing emojis, for example, can show that a very short reply is meant as a joke.\nG This is why a quick text can sometimes create more anxiety than comfort, despite its convenience.",[]],["e22fd06e-d287-44ae-8b54-607d3bca39bf","33f01a0bdcc0a69905a46e6a74e0841e","368864f19797319fa330346495c17a38","eec41a8937c6e619635c45c4e6a09313","eec41a8937c6e619635c45c4e6a09313","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who was motivated to change partly because of a financial consideration?\n44 Who thinks convenience is sometimes used as an excuse for keeping less sustainable habits?\n45 Who values an unexpected improvement in relationships with other people?\n46 Who has set up practical aids to avoid drifting back to how they used to live?\n47 Who says they avoid judging themselves for not managing to do everything perfectly?\n48 Who was influenced by younger family members to reconsider daily travel habits?\n49 Who became more concerned after taking part in a single event in the local area?\n50 Who dislikes the way some people treat green living as a chance to compare themselves with others?\n51 Who is unconvinced by products presented as necessary for an environmentally responsible lifestyle?\n52 Who discovered that one habit became straightforward once they had learnt some basic skills?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Leah Morgan\nAt first, I thought trying to live more sustainably meant making huge sacrifices, so I kept putting it off. Then my electricity bill rose sharply, and that finally pushed me to act. I started with ordinary habits: switching devices off properly, washing clothes at lower temperatures and carrying a flask instead of buying takeaway coffee. None of it felt dramatic, yet after a few months the changes became routine. Meanwhile, my neighbours set up a chat group where we exchange tips, and that has helped me stick to my plans. I still fly to visit my brother once a year, so I would never claim to be perfect. However, I have stopped worrying about getting every decision right. For me, the biggest lesson is that practical changes matter more than guilt, especially when they save money as well.\n________________________________________\nB – Nikhil Patel\nMy interest in climate action began for a different reason. I work as a chef, so I see every day how much food gets thrown away in homes and restaurants. Initially, I focused on composting and planning meals better, but since then I have become more ambitious. These days, I cycle to work most mornings, even though the traffic can be unpleasant, and I have cut down sharply on meat. Friends sometimes assume I enjoy setting strict rules for myself, yet that is not really true. In fact, I get annoyed when people turn small changes into a competition about who is the greenest. Even so, I do think convenience is often just an excuse. Once I learnt a few basic recipes and started using leftovers creatively, wasting less food turned out to be easier than I had expected.\n________________________________________\nC – Sophie Green\nI came to everyday climate action through my children. They returned from school full of ideas and immediately questioned why our family drove such short distances. To be honest, I resisted at first because my timetable already felt impossible. Since then, though, walking more and borrowing things instead of buying them have changed how I think about daily life. What surprised me most was the social side of it. Our street now runs occasional repair evenings, and people who barely spoke before now share tools and fix lamps, zips and bikes together. That sense of community matters to me as much as the environmental benefit. I am still doubtful about some trends online, especially expensive products marketed as essential for a greener lifestyle. If an action creates pressure to spend more, I usually question whether it is really the best solution.\n________________________________________\nD – Owen Kerr\nFor years I read articles about the climate and agreed with them in theory, yet I carried on living exactly as before. What changed was joining a local river-cleaning day. It was only a Saturday morning, but seeing piles of litter beside water I had known since childhood made the issue feel immediate rather than abstract. Since then, I have tried to reduce packaging, use public transport more often and buy fewer new clothes. I am not naturally organised, so I need systems: a shopping list, a reusable bag kept by the door, and reminders on my phone. Without those, I slip back into old habits surprisingly fast. I also think some campaigns ask too much from individuals while ignoring larger companies. Even so, taking action myself has stopped me feeling powerless, which is why I encourage other people to begin somewhere, however small.\n________________________________________",[]],["1357271b-d547-4d55-89fc-43a243c60b3b","b52589ec9776d5939c35d1861a9a92c8","bf2a38b744e7a9a9b296245df32698df","39bbaae9b8cb4369cd324bcda76a5b04","012997520e5e2e525008b9def9c32ece","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nLiving Beyond Earth\nFor decades, space travel was presented as a heroic adventure lasting a few thrilling days. Future missions are likely to be very different. A journey to Mars, for example, could take many months, with the crew living in a metal structure no bigger than a small flat. In that situation, success will depend not only on rockets and fuel, but also on whether people can stay healthy, motivated and cooperative when there is no quick way home.\nThat is why modern mission planning pays almost as much attention to ordinary daily life as to engineering. Researchers now study what astronauts eat, how they sleep and even how they argue. On the International Space Station, these questions matter, but support from Earth is always relatively close. For crews heading much farther away, however, every routine task becomes more important. If the coffee machine breaks in orbit around Earth, it is irritating; if the water recycling system fails halfway to Mars, it may threaten the whole mission.\nFood offers a good example of this change in thinking. Early space meals were designed simply to be safe, light and easy to store. Future crews will still need practical food, but they may also need meals that provide comfort and variety. Some scientists are testing ways of growing salad leaves and herbs in small onboard gardens, not because astronauts cannot survive without fresh flavour, but because familiar smells and colours may improve mood. A handful of green plants might therefore do more than supply vitamins: it could remind the crew that life exists beyond plastic walls and computer screens.\nPsychologists are equally interested in how groups behave over time. Films often show dramatic personal conflicts in space, yet real danger may come from quieter problems: boredom, tiredness and tiny habits that become unbearable after months together. In isolation studies on Earth, people have reported that the hardest part was not technical work but the lack of privacy and the feeling that every action was being observed. This suggests that future spacecraft will need not only excellent equipment but also carefully designed private corners where crew members can briefly escape one another.\nCommunication with Earth will also change. At great distances, messages can take many minutes to arrive, making ordinary conversation impossible. As a result, astronauts will have to solve more problems without immediate advice from mission control. This does not mean that experts on Earth will become unimportant. Rather, the balance of responsibility may shift. Space agencies have traditionally trained astronauts to follow detailed procedures, but future crews may need greater freedom to adapt when reality does not match the manual.\nEven leisure, once treated as a minor issue, is becoming part of serious planning. Engineers have considered virtual windows, recorded natural sounds and digital libraries so that astronauts can choose how to relax. Critics sometimes dismiss such ideas as luxuries, arguing that every kilogram launched into space should have a clear practical use. Yet this view assumes that mental wellbeing is somehow separate from mission performance. In fact, a crew that can rest properly and recover from stress may be safer and more efficient than one supplied only with the bare essentials.\nNone of this guarantees that life on a long mission will be comfortable. Space will remain cramped, risky and deeply unfamiliar. But the image of astronauts as fearless machine operators is fading. The more experts learn about long-distance travel, the clearer it becomes that future missions will depend on human needs that are surprisingly down to Earth. In the end, reaching another planet may require us to think less about escaping ordinary life and more about how to carry the best parts of it with us.\n\nQuestions\n31. What is the writer's main point in the first two paragraphs?\nA. Long space missions will succeed only if technical systems are kept as simple as possible.\nB. Future space travel will depend as much on managing human life as on advanced technology.\nC. Missions beyond Earth orbit are less attractive because everyday tasks become too difficult.\nD. Scientists are now more interested in astronauts' behaviour than in building spacecraft.\n32. What does the contrast between the coffee machine and the water recycling system illustrate?\nA. The systems that support ordinary life can be critical to the success of a long mission.\nB. Keeping crews comfortable is generally more difficult than keeping essential equipment working.\nC. A crew will normally find it harder to repair everyday devices than to maintain essential systems.\nD. Distance from Earth can make all types of breakdown equally serious.\n33. What additional benefit might an onboard garden bring to a long mission?\nA. It could make stored food unnecessary on some missions.\nB. It could give crews a way to test whether they are getting enough vitamins.\nC. Familiar plants could lift the crew’s mood in an artificial environment.\nD. Fresh ingredients could make it easier to keep meals small and light.\n34. Which less obvious source of tension does the writer mention for a crew on a long mission?\nA. Crew members refusing to follow safety procedures.\nB. Open arguments caused by stressful technical work.\nC. A lack of trust between astronauts and researchers on Earth.\nD. Small irritations becoming harder to tolerate over time.\n35. What is the writer's attitude towards giving future astronauts more independence from mission control?\nA. doubtful, because crews should still rely mainly on Earth-based experts\nB. supportive, because delays will make complete dependence on Earth unrealistic\nC. surprised, because astronauts have never had to make important decisions before\nD. unconcerned, because communication technology will soon remove any delays\n36. In the final paragraph, what does the writer mean by 'human needs that are surprisingly down to Earth'?\nA. People in space will eventually lose interest in exploring other planets.\nB. Astronauts will need training that is similar to ordinary office work.\nC. The basic comforts and routines of normal life remain important even in space.\nD. Space travel will become common only when it is affordable for everyone.",[["32 A) To show that some equipment problems become far more serious on distant missions.","32 A) The systems that support ordinary life can be critical to the success of a long mission.",true],["32 B) To suggest that astronauts in Earth orbit already face unnecessary discomfort.","32 B) Keeping crews comfortable is generally more difficult than keeping essential equipment working.",false],["32 C) To explain why water will replace other drinks on future spacecraft.","32 C) A crew will normally find it harder to repair everyday devices than to maintain essential systems.",false],["32 D) To criticise designers for paying attention to unimportant machinery.","32 D) Distance from Earth can make all types of breakdown equally serious.",false],["33 A) Fresh food will probably replace stored meals completely.","33 A) It could make stored food unnecessary on some missions.",false],["33 B) Scientists have proved that astronauts work better after gardening.","33 B) It could give crews a way to test whether they are getting enough vitamins.",false],["33 C) Plants may be valuable mainly because of their psychological effect.","33 C) Familiar plants could lift the crew’s mood in an artificial environment.",true],["33 D) Space agencies are more concerned with appearance than nutrition.","33 D) Fresh ingredients could make it easier to keep meals small and light.",false]]],["c7791afa-d569-4f0b-ae16-9bea6076f3e9","1c7f900828760238fcd10915272a5b89","1affce8e4b9c79288d943c08706b3f63","b5ce9eb0bc70aa541f5ab360aa527914","b5ce9eb0bc70aa541f5ab360aa527914","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who began using trains for most domestic trips after a storm left them stranded at an airport?\n44 Who suggests that where they live has a strong influence on which option they normally choose?\n45 Who appreciates being able to get on with work while travelling?\n46 Who admits to liking rail travel for emotional reasons rather than purely sensible ones?\n47 Who describes a single rail trip as the experience that persuaded them to reconsider their habits?\n48 Who mentions that travelling with other people affects their decision?\n49 Who feels that the total cost difference between the two methods is sometimes smaller than it first appears?\n50 Who believes that travel should involve noticing the places between the start and the finish?\n51 Who says a hypothetical improvement would make them change transport for many trips?\n52 Who concedes that their preferred method makes greater demands on the traveller beforehand?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Liam Porter\nI used to book flights without thinking twice, mainly because my work involved short notice meetings in different cities. Initially, I saw trains as the slower option for people with time to spare. That changed after a winter storm left me sleeping on an airport bench while the rail services were mostly back to normal by morning. Since then, I still fly for some journeys abroad, yet for trips within the country I nearly always choose the train. I can open my laptop, answer emails and arrive feeling as if the day has already started, rather than as if I have survived an obstacle course. It is usually pricier than the budget flights my colleagues hunt down, although once I add airport transfers and parking, the gap shrinks. For me, the calmer experience matters as much as the timetable.\n________________________________________\nB – Sonia Malik\nMy choice between rail and air is largely practical rather than ideological. I live in a town with a small airport twenty minutes away, while the nearest mainline station is a bus ride and a long wait from my front door. Because of that, flying often wins for family visits, especially when I am travelling with two energetic children and several bags. People assume airports are stressful, but I find them manageable if I check in online and keep expectations realistic. However, I would never claim planes are always better. Last summer, after a delayed departure and a missed connection, I spent more than twelve hours getting to somewhere that is only six by train. Meanwhile, the children were exhausted. If a direct rail service existed from where I live, I suspect I would switch immediately for many journeys.\n________________________________________\nC – Harriet Doyle\nI am one of those people who genuinely enjoy the journey itself, so trains suit me perfectly. Looking out of the window helps me settle into a trip in a way flying never has. At airports, everything seems designed to hurry you from one queue to another, and I arrive already irritated. By contrast, on trains I read, daydream or chat to whoever is beside me. That said, I am not romantic about it. I have had cramped seats, broken air conditioning and one memorable evening when we stopped in open countryside for two hours with no explanation. Even so, I remain loyal to rail because it feels connected to real places instead of sealed off from them. Friends tell me I should take cheaper flights for weekend breaks, yet I would rather spend longer travelling than give up that sense of gradual arrival.\n________________________________________\nD – Noah Greene\nUntil recently, I had hardly travelled by train beyond my daily commute. I assumed long-distance rail would be unreliable and old-fashioned, so for years I booked flights automatically, especially for city breaks in Europe. Then my partner persuaded me to try an overnight train to the continent instead of flying. Frankly, I expected to regret it. Yet the trip turned out to be the highlight of the holiday: we boarded in the evening, had dinner, slept surprisingly well and woke up in the centre of our destination. Since then, I have become far more interested in planning routes that avoid airports altogether, although I know this approach only works if you organise well in advance. The one drawback is that rail journeys sometimes demand more research and flexibility. Even so, I now see travel time as part of the experience rather than wasted hours to be cut.\n________________________________________",[]],["3f9a02cd-133a-4f64-a73f-fda1f101975c","fae113ae99f80dc8c5f5cb471077ff57","30b42bf887475033923896c66e2fe082","3b4cba6029504d76a0c37dc6d4c35d3f","3b4cba6029504d76a0c37dc6d4c35d3f","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nWhat the Sea Keeps Hidden\nFor centuries, the deep sea was imagined as an empty and rather lifeless place. Divers could only reach shallow water, and early maps showed huge areas of ocean floor as blank spaces. That picture has changed dramatically in the last few decades, as better submarines, cameras and sensors have allowed researchers to explore further than ever before. (37) As a result, the seabed is now seen not as a flat desert, but as a varied landscape full of life, movement and surprising discoveries.\nOne of the most dramatic moments came when scientists first found hydrothermal vents in the Pacific Ocean. These are cracks in the seabed where extremely hot water, rich in minerals, pours out from below the Earth's surface. (38) Yet around these dark openings, researchers discovered thick communities of tube worms, crabs and bacteria that were thriving without any sunlight at all.\nThat finding forced biologists to rethink one of their most basic assumptions. Until then, nearly all known ecosystems were thought to depend, directly or indirectly, on energy from the Sun. In vent communities, however, bacteria gain energy from chemicals released by the Earth itself, and other creatures feed on them. (39) It also raised new questions about whether similar forms of life might exist on other planets or moons with oceans beneath ice.\nDiscoveries beneath the sea are not limited to living things. Shipwrecks, tools and even entire lost settlements have been found underwater, preserved in mud or cold, dark conditions. Marine archaeologists say the sea can act like a giant storage room, protecting objects that would quickly rot or break apart on land. (40) In some cases, a wreck does more than reveal a single disaster: it can offer a detailed picture of trade, diet and daily routine in a past society.\nModern technology has made this kind of work far more precise. Remotely operated vehicles can stay underwater for hours, collecting samples and sending back high-quality images. Powerful scanning systems can also build detailed maps of the seabed before anyone decides where to explore more closely. (41) Instead of relying on chance, research teams can now compare data, identify unusual shapes and return to exact spots again and again.\nHowever, greater access brings new problems. Deep-sea tourism, fishing and plans for underwater mining are expanding into places that were once difficult to reach. Scientists worry that habitats could be damaged before they are properly understood, especially because many deep-sea species grow slowly and may take decades to recover. (42) For that reason, several research groups are calling for stronger international rules to protect vulnerable areas before commercial activity increases.\nThe sea still covers more than two thirds of the planet, and much of it remains unexplored. Each expedition adds a few more details to a picture that is still incomplete. What lies below is not simply a collection of strange creatures or sunken objects, but a record of how Earth works and how people have lived on it. The more we discover, the clearer it becomes that the ocean floor is one of the world's last great frontiers.\n\nSentences\nA This changed ideas not only about the deep ocean, but about the possible limits of life itself.\nB Such objects are often surprisingly ordinary: bowls, ropes and even the grain a ship was carrying.\nC The first images of these vents contradicted what many researchers had expected to find in such heat and darkness.\nD Every new descent has brought back images and samples that nobody had predicted.\nE This has transformed underwater research from a largely uncertain search into something much more systematic.\nF Once damage is done in the deep ocean, it may be almost impossible to reverse.\nG By contrast, many coastal towns now use underwater museums to attract visitors during the summer season.",[]],["9d6fa49b-825d-4c5f-8b4a-d1747bb7a9ef","f52a687794fdf20a8cbcb5ed46a9b8c2","789e16a591bb53f1dcf19bbff5053495","06826fdcece422a903944550d5dbcf3c","06826fdcece422a903944550d5dbcf3c","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who realised unexpectedly that reading could improve a period of time they had usually treated as unimportant?\n44 Who thinks the growth in reading is partly connected to financial pressures?\n45 Who mentions changing their choice of reading material in order to make the habit easier to continue?\n46 Who suggests that interest in books has increased because people want relief from digital overload?\n47 Who noticed that people increasingly value discussing books together, even when they are not fully prepared?\n48 Who is doubtful about a fashionable way some people present themselves as readers?\n49 Who feels that social contact linked to reading can still have a positive long-term effect?\n50 Who was mistaken at first about what was attracting people to book-related content?\n51 Who argues that people must actively make room for reading if it is to become a lasting habit?\n52 Who was surprised that younger people preferred personal advice to online opinion when choosing what to read?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Leila Grant, bookseller\nA few years ago, customers mainly came into my shop asking for gifts, while they bought most of their own reading online. Initially, I assumed that habit had changed for good. However, things have shifted again. Since the pandemic, people seem less interested in rushing through a screen all day and more willing to spend an evening with a novel. What surprises me most is that many younger customers ask staff for recommendations instead of relying on star ratings. They often tell me that reading feels like a form of rest rather than another task. I have also noticed people joining our monthly discussions even when they have not finished the book, simply because they want company. Sales matter, of course, yet what really convinces me something wider is happening is the atmosphere: the shop is noisier, slower and more social than it used to be.\n________________________________________\nB – Owen Price, engineer\nI never thought of myself as a reader. At school I associated books with tests, and later I told myself I was too busy. Then, during a long train journey when my phone battery died, I started a paperback that had been sitting in my bag for months. By the time I arrived, I had forgotten to check messages. Since then, reading has become part of my commute. I still use technology all day for work, so opening a book feels refreshingly different. That said, I do not romanticise it. I choose crime fiction because it holds my attention, not because I want to become more cultured. Some friends have taken up reading to post impressive shelves online, which seems odd to me. For me, the appeal is simpler: books make dead time feel properly used, and they leave my head quieter than podcasts do.\n________________________________________\nC – Marta Silva, librarian\nFrom where I work, the return to reading is real, but it is not quite the cosy story people imagine. Yes, our membership has risen steadily, and teenagers now borrow printed books as well as manga and audiobooks. Meanwhile, parents often say they are relieved to see their children choosing anything that is not a phone. Even so, I think the biggest reason is practical. Household budgets are tight, and libraries offer free entertainment, warmth and a reason to leave the house. Book clubs have filled up too, although many members admit they come as much for conversation as for literature. I do not see that as a weakness. In fact, shared reading can lead people towards more challenging authors later on. Still, I would be cautious about calling this a golden age. Enthusiasm is growing, yet people need access, time and quiet if it is to last.\n________________________________________\nD – Rafi Khan, presenter\nWhen I started hosting a culture podcast, I expected film and music episodes to attract the biggest audiences. Strangely, the discussions about books are now downloaded far more. At first I assumed listeners wanted expert interviews, but the messages we receive suggest something else. People say they miss the concentration they once had, and hearing others talk enthusiastically about a title gives them the push to begin one themselves. I recognise that feeling. For years I bought serious books and left them unread beside my bed. Recently I stopped aiming to read what I thought I should read and chose shorter travel writing instead. That changed everything. Yet I am not convinced reading is returning because society has suddenly become calmer or wiser. If anything, people are seeking a way to resist constant distraction. Whether that habit survives depends on making room for it, not just admiring it from a distance.\n________________________________________",[]],["c6b245d3-9715-4525-b474-016c81175ce2","7c372ad0eabd16901e3b6fe0116aff6e","633bdee803df8bb12e562c470b3e45a5","bd0008f3e0a49e15ec5d8980bcd55b6c","7e5c5ff67da8c5dd6675425236dcce67","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nWhen the Weekend Job Becomes Your Real One\nPeople often say, half-jokingly, that they would happily do their hobby for a living if only someone would pay them. It sounds like a fantasy: spending each day baking, repairing bicycles, taking photographs or training dogs instead of answering emails in an office. Yet for a growing number of people, this is not simply a daydream. Online platforms, specialist markets and social media have made it easier than ever to reach customers directly. At the same time, the idea of a single career for life has weakened, and many workers now expect to change direction at least once.\nEven so, the move from hobby to profession is rarely as simple as outsiders imagine. What begins as a relaxing escape from daily pressures can quickly acquire deadlines, invoices and awkward customer requests. The ceramic artist who once enjoyed shaping bowls at midnight whenever inspiration appeared may suddenly need to produce thirty matching mugs by Friday. A guitarist who loved experimenting alone may find that teaching beginners pays the bills better than performing does. In other words, earning money from a hobby does not just add income; it changes the activity itself.\nThat shift can be uncomfortable, especially at the start. Some new business owners discover that the part they most enjoyed is now surrounded by tasks they had never considered. Selling handmade soap, for instance, may involve packaging rules, photography, stock records and constant communication with buyers. There is also the emotional difficulty of attaching a price to something personal. Charging friends for portraits or asking strangers to pay for cakes based on family recipes can feel less like business and more like self-exposure. For that reason, people who succeed are not always the most talented. Often, they are the ones who can tolerate uncertainty and keep going while they learn unfamiliar skills.\nFinancially, caution matters. Stories about overnight success attract attention, but they hide the slower reality. Many people begin by working evenings and weekends, testing whether customers will return before leaving a secure salary. That approach can be exhausting, but it provides useful information. It reveals whether the product is genuinely wanted, whether prices cover costs and whether enthusiasm survives repetition. A person may love making jewellery, for example, yet feel very differently after producing the same popular design fifty times in one month. Better to discover that while the hobby is still optional than after rent depends on it.\nThere is another less obvious challenge: identity. When people ask what you do, saying \"I'm trying to make a living from restoring old furniture\" can sound uncertain even if the business is developing well. Society still tends to respect conventional job titles more than self-created ones, particularly during the messy middle stage when income is irregular. Family members may worry that a sensible career is being thrown away, while former colleagues assume the change is temporary. Such reactions can make people doubt themselves, not because the new path is wrong, but because it has fewer recognised milestones.\nNone of this means turning a hobby into a career is a mistake. For some, it leads to a more satisfying working life precisely because it combines personal interest with independence. But the most successful people are usually those who treat passion as a starting point, not a guarantee. They accept that professionalising a hobby means protecting what they love while also submitting it to market forces. The reward is not endless enjoyment untouched by pressure. It is the chance to build work that feels meaningful, provided they are willing to see it clearly, not romantically.\n\nQuestions\n31. What point does the writer make in the first two paragraphs about turning a hobby into a career?\nA. It has become more possible, but it changes the nature of the activity.\nB. It is mainly attractive to people who are tired of office life.\nC. It usually succeeds because online tools remove most of the risks.\nD. It works best when the hobby already involves dealing with other people.\n32. In the third paragraph, what does the writer suggest about people who manage this kind of career change well?\nA. They are comfortable promoting themselves in public.\nB. They are realistic about having to learn beyond their original hobby.\nC. They have usually worked in business before.\nD. They avoid charging people they know for their work.\n33. Why does the writer mention making the same jewellery design many times in one month?\nA. To show that customer demand can rise faster than expected.\nB. To argue that creative work is harder to price than practical work.\nC. To illustrate how repeating a successful item may reduce the pleasure of making it.\nD. To explain why jewellery is a risky hobby to turn into a business.\n34. How does the writer view the doubts that family members and former colleagues express?\nA. They suggest that the business is unlikely to survive its first year.\nB. They are a helpful test of whether someone can deal with customers.\nC. They can undermine confidence even when the new venture is developing well.\nD. They matter mostly when the person has yet to earn any money from the hobby.\n35. What does the writer mean by saying that an unconventional career has ‘fewer recognised milestones’?\nA. There are fewer familiar signs by which people can judge how far they have progressed.\nB. The financial risks decrease more quickly than they do in a conventional career.\nC. People are generally promoted earlier when they work for themselves.\nD. The work feels less rewarding until former colleagues begin to take it seriously.\n36. Which statement best summarises the writer's overall message in the article?\nA. A hobby should remain separate from paid work unless demand is guaranteed.\nB. Creative hobbies are easier to turn into careers than practical ones.\nC. The main difficulty is persuading other people to take the new career seriously.\nD. Passion can lead to satisfying work, but only if it is supported by realism and adaptability.",[["34 A) amused that they are often based on jealousy","34 A) They suggest that the business is unlikely to survive its first year.",false],["34 B) critical of their failure to notice creative talent","34 B) They are a helpful test of whether someone can deal with customers.",false],["34 C) sympathetic to the pressure they can put on someone","34 C) They can undermine confidence even when the new venture is developing well.",true],["34 D) surprised that they remain so negative nowadays","34 D) They matter mostly when the person has yet to earn any money from the hobby.",false],["35 A) They should think realistically about both the rewards and the difficulties of turning a hobby into a career.","35 A) There are fewer familiar signs by which people can judge how far they have progressed.",true],["35 B) They should stop treating the hobby as enjoyable once they begin earning money from it.","35 B) The financial risks decrease more quickly than they do in a conventional career.",false],["35 C) They should try to protect the hobby completely from market pressures and customer expectations.","35 C) People are generally promoted earlier when they work for themselves.",false],["35 D) They should only turn a hobby into a career if other people consider the decision sensible.","35 D) The work feels less rewarding until former colleagues begin to take it seriously.",false]]],["77737565-5f43-499f-b12c-7adc2f69f3dc","d30e2f2ddfa7b52a670a89b942b26ff0","3a058050b11b8d25c51613db61e08540","0fc4745cbe2a76486254c1ebcddd3c4a","0fc4745cbe2a76486254c1ebcddd3c4a","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person...? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who changed their opinion after using smart technology in difficult personal circumstances?\n44 Who decided that a device should not be allowed to affect time away from their job?\n45 Who mentions that one piece of technology turned out to require more effort than a basic alternative?\n46 Who believes smart products are worthwhile only when they deal with an actual everyday problem?\n47 Who says they became aware of a health-related issue because of information collected by a device?\n48 Who bought a smart device because they were missing messages while cycling to work?\n49 Who is careful not to accept the idea that the most modern option is automatically the best one?\n50 Who suggests that experience with many devices has made them harder to impress?\n51 Who mentions taking action to reduce interruptions caused by a device?\n52 Who values a device mainly because it reduces the need to depend on other people?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Rosa Mendez\nI used to laugh at people who checked their phones every few minutes, yet now I rely on a smartwatch more than I expected. I bought it initially because I kept missing messages while cycling across town to my bakery. Since then, though, it has quietly changed my routine in other ways. The reminders to stand up or drink water are useful, and paying for ingredients with a tap feels quicker than searching for coins with flour on my hands. However, I switched off most notifications after a few weeks. I realised I was letting tiny vibrations interrupt conversations with customers. My brother says these devices make life easier only if you control them, and I think he is right. I still enjoy the health tracking, but I refuse to wear the watch in the evening. Home should feel different from work.\n________________________________________\nB – Gareth Cole\nFor me, smart devices became essential during a long recovery after a knee operation. Before that, I had seen them as expensive toys. A friend persuaded me to try a voice-controlled speaker and a sleep monitor, and I was surprised by how much independence they gave me. From bed, I could adjust the lights, listen to the news and even make shopping lists without asking for help. Meanwhile, the sleep data helped me notice a pattern I would otherwise have ignored: pain medication was affecting my rest. That said, I am uneasy about how much personal information these gadgets collect. I read every privacy setting carefully, although I know most people never bother. Even so, I have kept the devices because they solved practical problems at a time when I really needed support, and they still save me effort now.\n________________________________________\nC – Leila Hassan\nAs a secondary school teacher, I spend all day asking teenagers to put their phones away, so friends find it amusing that my flat is full of connected devices. Still, I am selective about what deserves space in my life. I love my smart thermostat because it learns when I am usually home and stops me wasting money on heating. By contrast, I returned a robot vacuum after a month. It was entertaining at first, yet it constantly got stuck under a chair and demanded more attention than an ordinary broom. What annoys me most is the assumption that newer always means better. Sometimes the simplest option is still the most sensible one. Even so, I admit that when my train is delayed and my lights turn on automatically before I arrive, it feels oddly reassuring rather than unnecessary.\n________________________________________\nD – Noah Price\nI review consumer technology for a local website, so people expect me to fill my house with the latest devices. Actually, I try to live with only a few, because testing too many products has made me less easily impressed. At first, every new feature seems clever; later, you notice how many are solutions to problems nobody had. The exception for me is a tiny tracker attached to my keys and wallet. I travel constantly for work, and before buying it I wasted ridiculous amounts of time searching hotel rooms and train seats. Now I can locate things in seconds, which lowers my stress more than any fancy entertainment system could. Yet I never recommend buying smart devices simply because they are fashionable. If a product does not remove a real frustration from your day, the excitement usually disappears fast.\n________________________________________",[]],["bbf079a0-6402-493d-b2db-8013fc5ce65c","251fc4ae998edd882a84a1d3f0b7dd0b","9bac7b238ddfe332c54edb99a8fe9fad","0931d7acd8ae7e6003f1e3b6e9a89567","b6bdee47333efe719b296e4d0b33ac6a","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nThe Things We Never Planned to Buy\nMost of us know the feeling. We go into a shop for toothpaste and come out carrying a candle, a notebook and a packet of biscuits as well. We click online to replace a phone charger and somehow end up paying for trainers we had not even considered a minute earlier. Later, when the excitement has gone, we may wonder why we wanted any of it. Yet this pattern is so common that businesses spend huge amounts trying to understand it. Their success does not depend only on making useful products. It also relies on recognising the small psychological nudges that can turn a passing glance into a purchase.\nOne of the strongest nudges is the hope that an object might improve not just our lives but our identities. People do not only buy running shoes because they need something on their feet. They may also be buying a picture of themselves as healthier, busier or more disciplined. The same thing happens with kitchen equipment, expensive pens and travel bags. A product can seem to promise a future version of us, and that future can be more attractive than the object itself. This helps explain why unopened items often gather dust in cupboards: they were bought for the person we meant to become, not the one we already were.\nShops and websites are skilled at making that imagined future feel close. Online stores, for example, recommend items by suggesting that people similar to us chose them, too. This can be useful, but it also removes some of the pause in which we might ask whether we need the item at all. Physical shops do something similar through layout. Essentials are rarely near the entrance, so customers travel past rows of things they never intended to see. None of this forces anyone to buy. But the environment quietly increases the number of moments in which buying feels easy and saying no feels like an unnecessary effort.\nAnother reason we overspend is that modern shopping often separates the pleasure of choosing from the pain of paying. Cash once made the cost visible and immediate. Now many purchases happen with a quick tap, and online payments may be completed before we have properly noticed the total. Subscription services push this even further. A small monthly charge can feel harmless, even if several of them together cost far more than a single larger payment would. People are not foolish for missing this. Our brains tend to notice immediate rewards more clearly than slow, repeated losses.\nEmotions matter just as much. Buying can create a brief sense of control, comfort or reward, especially after a difficult day. That is why boredom, stress and loneliness are such powerful shopping partners. The item itself may be almost irrelevant; what matters is the lift that comes from deciding, receiving and opening. Companies are well aware of this and often design the process to feel smooth and satisfying, from attractive packaging to messages announcing that an order is on its way. These details may appear minor, but together they turn shopping into an experience rather than a simple exchange.\nNone of this means that people are helpless or that all unplanned buying is a mistake. Pleasure has value, and an unexpected purchase can sometimes be a good one. The problem starts when buying becomes our automatic response to every mood or every clever suggestion. The most effective solution is not usually strict self-denial. It is to create enough delay for thought to return: leaving an online basket overnight, unsubscribing from marketing emails, or deciding in advance what kind of shopper we want to be. The aim is not to remove enjoyment from spending, but to make sure our money follows our decisions instead of somebody else's design.\n\nQuestions\n31. What is the writer doing in the article as a whole?\nA. warning readers that modern products are usually poor value\nB. explaining why people often buy more than they intended\nC. arguing that online shopping has replaced shopping in stores\nD. suggesting that advertising should be controlled more strictly\n32. In the second paragraph, what point does the writer make about items such as running shoes and travel bags?\nA. People often choose them because they are practical and long-lasting.\nB. They are bought mainly to match what friends and colleagues own.\nC. They can appeal because of the kind of person buyers hope to become.\nD. Buyers usually regret spending too much on goods they rarely use.\n33. Why does the writer mention the layout of physical shops in the third paragraph?\nA. to show one way in which businesses increase opportunities for impulse buying\nB. to argue that customers should avoid large shops whenever possible\nC. to explain why essential goods are usually more expensive than other items\nD. to suggest that shoppers are more easily influenced in stores than online\n34. According to the fourth paragraph, why can subscription services encourage people to spend too much?\nA. They persuade people to pay in cash more often than they expect.\nB. They make it difficult for people to compare brands properly.\nC. They offer immediate rewards that disappear very quickly.\nD. They hide the overall cost behind charges that seem small on their own.\n35. Why might someone buy something they had no intention of getting after a difficult day?\nA. Choosing a product helps them work out what they need most urgently.\nB. Making a purchase can briefly restore a feeling of control or comfort.\nC. An unplanned order often costs less than one they have considered for a long time.\nD. They may be trying to make up for an earlier purchase they regret.\n36. What conclusion does the writer reach in the final paragraph?\nA. Unplanned purchases are best prevented by making strict rules about spending.\nB. Enjoying occasional impulse buys is harmless if income is high enough.\nC. The key is to build in time to think before deciding to buy.\nD. People should stop companies from collecting information about their habits.",[["35 A) impatient, because they should have learnt from past mistakes","35 A) Choosing a product helps them work out what they need most urgently.",false],["35 B) sympathetic, because this reflects a normal human tendency","35 B) Making a purchase can briefly restore a feeling of control or comfort.",true],["35 C) doubtful, because many people exaggerate how little they realise","35 C) An unplanned order often costs less than one they have considered for a long time.",false],["35 D) amused, because shopping habits are often slightly ridiculous","35 D) They may be trying to make up for an earlier purchase they regret.",false]]],["7177ae00-584c-44a1-833c-ae2c6e08f2d7","075518fb00b849a273ce7123d0c4a507","6af0210715c923d6f3641e288476ae61","61fba9d5b10be6037cd2b0b85f61f15a","61fba9d5b10be6037cd2b0b85f61f15a","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nEating Well for Less\nFor many people, healthy eating seems to belong in the same category as luxury gym memberships and expensive kitchen gadgets. Fresh ingredients are often seen as something for shoppers with plenty of time and money. Yet nutrition experts repeatedly point out that eating well does not depend on buying the most fashionable products in the supermarket. (37) Once that idea changes, planning cheaper and healthier meals becomes much more realistic.\nOne reason healthy food appears costly is that people compare individual items rather than whole meals. A ready-made pizza may look cheaper than buying vegetables, rice and chicken separately. However, the pizza usually feeds fewer people and leaves nothing for the next day. (38) In other words, the better comparison is not product against product, but meal against meal.\nPlanning is one of the simplest ways to control food spending. People who decide what they will cook before shopping are less likely to buy snacks, duplicate ingredients or food they never use. A short list also helps shoppers ignore special offers that are not really useful. (39) That way, ingredients bought for one dish can often be used in another later in the week.\nSome of the best-value foods are ones many shoppers walk past without much thought. Dried lentils, oats, eggs, frozen peas and tinned tomatoes are rarely advertised as exciting, but they are versatile and nutritious. They can become soups, pasta sauces, curries or quick lunches with very little effort. (40) Choosing a few reliable basics like these can reduce both cost and stress.\nAnother useful habit is being flexible about where nutrition comes from. Many people build meals around meat and then add vegetables to the side. That can work, but it is often the most expensive approach. Beans, chickpeas and eggs provide protein at a much lower price, and they can still produce filling meals. (41) The point is not to give up meat completely, but to use it more carefully.\nOf course, even careful shoppers waste money if food ends up in the bin. Leftovers are not exciting, which is why they are often forgotten until it is too late. But a small amount of cooked rice, roast vegetables or chicken can easily become lunch the next day. (42) Besides saving money, this habit also saves the time and energy needed to cook from the beginning again.\nIn the end, healthy eating on a budget is less about finding one perfect shopping trick and more about building several sensible habits. None of them is especially complicated: compare meals, plan ahead, keep basic ingredients at home and use what you buy. Taken together, those choices can make home cooking cheaper than many people expect. They also show that eating well is not reserved for people with unusually large food budgets.\n\nSentences\nA In fact, meals based on ordinary ingredients are often cheaper than heavily processed convenience food.\nB A large pot of soup or curry, for example, can provide several portions at a reasonable cost.\nC It is often smarter to think about how a week of meals will fit together before entering the shop.\nD They also last for a long time, so there is less pressure to use them immediately.\nE Meat can then be used in smaller amounts, for instance to add flavour to a dish built around beans.\nF With a little imagination, those leftovers can be turned into something that feels like a new meal.\nG Some shoppers save money by visiting several different supermarkets in a single afternoon.",[]],["df6533b0-4820-4ab9-8977-66b5ab359dc1","dd047368d10790ebe867bfaf8f97439d","dd39547249cf76ae6d4ee18a5849dd70","bd354adbfc19dbae87f658a06e032410","bd354adbfc19dbae87f658a06e032410","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who says the lack of consultation with residents bothers them more than the number of visitors?\n44 Who admits that contact with outsiders changed their own attitude towards the place where they live?\n45 Who is concerned that young people may make limited plans because of jobs linked to visitors?\n46 Who suggests that public anger reduced when residents noticed practical improvements?\n47 Who says tourism led to worthwhile investment after pressure from people visiting the area?\n48 Who feels that an activity once valued mainly by residents has lost some of its original character?\n49 Who thinks the key question is not the presence of tourists, but who benefits financially?\n50 Who accepts that tourism brings advantages, while regretting a weaker sense of community?\n51 Who argues that local people should stay involved because tourism is unlikely to disappear?\n52 Who warns against allowing immediate economic needs to shape long-term decisions?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Luca Ferris, café owner\nWhen the first budget flights began using the airport nearby, our town changed almost overnight. Initially, I was delighted: I run a small café on the harbour, and extra visitors meant I could stay open all year instead of closing every winter. Since then, however, the success has had awkward side effects. Rents have risen so sharply that two of my staff now share a flat an hour away. I also miss recognising faces in the street. Yet I would be dishonest if I pretended tourism had brought nothing but problems. The council finally repaired the seafront because holidaymakers complained online, and local fishermen now sell directly to restaurants rather than through middlemen. What bothers me most is not the number of tourists, but the way decisions are made without asking those of us who live and work here.\n________________________________________\nB – Hannah Price, volunteer guide\nI moved to the village ten years ago for a quieter life, so I was sceptical when coaches started arriving every weekend. Meanwhile, the old mill, which had stood empty for years, was turned into a visitor centre, and I agreed to help there one afternoon a week. To my surprise, meeting travellers has made me appreciate the place more, because their questions force me to notice details I had ignored. Even so, I worry that some businesses now present a polished version of local life that residents barely recognise. The summer craft market, for instance, used to be where neighbours swapped homemade goods; now it can feel like a performance. Still, I would rather manage tourism carefully than try to stop it. People will keep coming, and if locals take part, we have a better chance of protecting what matters.\n________________________________________\nC – Sanjay Mehta, secondary school teacher\nOur mountain region has long depended on farming, but poor harvests pushed many families into tourist work. At first, I welcomed that shift because several parents finally had a stable income. Yet after a few seasons, I noticed something less obvious in school. Teenagers were leaving classes early to help in guesthouses, especially during peak months, and some no longer saw education as worthwhile. There is also pressure to learn only the kind of English needed for serving visitors, which seems far too narrow to me. On the other hand, tourism has funded a new sports hall that the whole community uses, and a few former students have returned after university to start walking tours with real respect for local traditions. So I am not against visitors; I simply think short-term earnings should not decide the region’s future.\n________________________________________\nD – Marta Zielińska, town councillor\nBy the time I joined the council, residents were already angry about crowded buses and noisy holiday flats, so any proposal linked to tourism was bound to cause arguments. I understood the frustration, yet I also knew the town could not rely on its declining fishing industry forever. We introduced limits on short-term rentals and changed rubbish collection times, which helped a little. What surprised me was how quickly tempers cooled once people saw that complaints led to action. Even now, though, I am uneasy when campaigners talk as if tourism itself were the enemy. In my view, the real issue is whether profits stay locally or disappear into companies based elsewhere. If more small firms were owned by residents, the conversation would be far less bitter. Since then, I have become convinced that control matters more than growth.\n________________________________________",[]],["0058157c-8565-4647-ab96-060cee3a6c7d","f71c81fb8edd6949bbc341a2ebc0ca57","4a038e8b80fcbe2a2c4df5f8686eda2f","f960bd33cc7863f4eca1fe49e9bbb3dc","f960bd33cc7863f4eca1fe49e9bbb3dc","Six sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nWhy Home Cooking Is Back\nFor years, the story of modern eating seemed simple: people were busier than ever, takeaway apps were growing fast, and cooking at home looked old-fashioned. Yet recently, the opposite trend has begun to attract attention. More people are planning meals, buying basic ingredients and sharing photos of dishes they have made themselves. It is not just a hobby for skilled food lovers, either. Ordinary households are starting to see cooking as a normal part of daily life again.\nOne reason is clearly financial. Meals bought outside the home have become noticeably more expensive, especially in towns and cities where food delivery is common. Families who once relied on prepared food a few nights a week are now doing the maths more carefully. (37) As a result, dishes that were once seen as slow or inconvenient, such as soups, pasta sauces and rice-based meals, suddenly make practical sense again.\nMoney, however, is only part of the explanation. During periods when people spent more time at home, many discovered that cooking could structure the day and provide a small sense of progress. Chopping vegetables or preparing dough offered a break from screens and constant messages. (38) For some, that confidence remained even after life became busier again, and they continued to cook because they now felt capable rather than nervous.\nThe internet has also changed the experience in ways that make home cooking easier to begin. In the past, a beginner might have been put off by a recipe full of unfamiliar terms or assumed knowledge. Now, short videos can show every step, from holding a knife correctly to checking whether onions are cooked enough. (39) That kind of guidance reduces the risk of wasting ingredients, which matters even more when budgets are tight.\nAnother important change involves ideas about health. People have become more aware of how often they eat highly processed food and how little control they have over what goes into it. At home, even simple cooking gives them more influence over salt, sugar and portion size. (40) Instead, they are aiming for meals that feel realistic on a weekday but still healthier than something bought in a hurry.\nThere is also a social side to the return of home cooking. In some households, preparing dinner has become a shared task rather than one person’s responsibility. Children wash vegetables, flatmates take turns choosing recipes, and friends invite one another over for low-cost meals. (41) It turns cooking into an activity with conversation and routine, rather than a lonely job that has to be finished as quickly as possible.\nOf course, not everyone has the time, space or energy to cook regularly, and ready-made food remains useful for many people. Even so, the renewed interest in home cooking seems likely to last. (42) In that sense, the return of home cooking is not really about going backwards. It is about finding a habit that suits modern life better than many people expected.\n\nSentences\nA Many people realised that a home-cooked meal could feed several people for the same price as a single order from a restaurant.\nB Greater control does not mean people expect to recreate the meals served in a restaurant.\nC In addition, viewers can pause, replay and compare their results with what they see on screen.\nD Sharing the preparation gives people a chance to talk before anyone even sits down to eat.\nE Successfully repeating one simple dish helped beginners feel that they could rely on their own judgement.\nF Even people who are not especially interested in food can see the advantage of a skill that saves money and offers a degree of control.\nG Some food companies have responded by reducing portion sizes and changing their packaging designs.",[]],["6a4fbbda-7df5-44ea-8805-3cc62ebede66","2b3af5763af4ae07b77873e722968036","585671c8c682664b7f463653a6d0f3a1","6d1cad3bd7bf34b732d34b11db2c2441","6d1cad3bd7bf34b732d34b11db2c2441","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person talks about each of the following? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who discovered through making things for an evening group that they wanted work with practical design problems to solve?\n44 Who says that outsiders fail to appreciate how important their work can be?\n45 Who advises people not to be too influenced by other people's expectations?\n46 Who mentions that their current role still makes use of knowledge gained earlier?\n47 Who chose a path mainly because their personal circumstances limited the alternatives?\n48 Who accepted a less stable income in exchange for feeling happier about work?\n49 Who discovered that the part of work they enjoyed most was not the one they were originally employed to do?\n50 Who stayed in a profession because they were good at it, even though they felt little connection with the work?\n51 Who talks about enjoying work because it combines routine with the unexpected?\n52 Who is considering becoming independent in their field at a later stage?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Leah Morton\nAt school I was the sort of person who wanted a plan for everything, so choosing a career felt strangely difficult. I did well in science and everyone assumed I would study medicine, yet I wasn't convinced it suited me. During a summer job at a local garden centre, I realised I enjoyed explaining things to customers far more than dealing with stock. That surprised me. Initially, I ignored it and started a pharmacy course because it seemed sensible. However, after six months I switched to training as a science teacher. Since then, I've never regretted it. The pay isn't amazing and the workload can be intense, especially in winter, but I like the mix of structure and unpredictability. Young people ask honest questions, and that keeps me interested. Looking back, I wish I'd paid less attention to what other people thought was impressive.\n________________________________________\nB – Ravi Sen\nMy route into work was less about passion and more about necessity. My family ran a small café, and as the oldest child I helped out from the age of thirteen. By the time I left school, I knew I could manage people and solve problems quickly, but university wasn't financially realistic. Meanwhile, one of our suppliers suggested I apply for an apprenticeship in logistics. I had no idea what the sector involved, yet I liked the fact that every day brought a different challenge. Ten years on, I coordinate deliveries for a food company. Some friends still think it sounds dull compared with creative jobs, although I disagree. If a shipment goes wrong, shops can end up with empty shelves, so the pressure is real. I may eventually set up my own business, but for now I'm learning as much as I can from experienced colleagues.\n________________________________________\nC – Nina Kowalska\nI spent years trying to choose between secure work and something more personal. As a teenager I loved drawing clothes, but people kept warning me that fashion was too competitive. In the end, I trained as an accountant and stayed in the profession longer than expected because I was good at it. Even so, I always felt slightly detached from what I was doing. Then, while making costumes for a local theatre group in the evenings, I noticed how absorbed I became when time was short and a design problem needed fixing. That was a clue I couldn't ignore. I now run a small alterations studio and design custom pieces at weekends. The income is less predictable than before, which can be worrying. Yet I prefer that uncertainty to spending every Monday counting the days until Friday. For me, choosing the right path meant accepting that comfort isn't the same as satisfaction.\n________________________________________\nD – Owen Price\nPeople often imagine that choosing a career is a single dramatic moment, but mine changed through a series of small decisions. I studied computer engineering and joined a large tech company straight after university. The office was modern, the salary was decent and the promotion route was clear. Still, after a while I found myself enjoying the training sessions more than the technical work. I liked helping new staff understand difficult systems and watching them become more confident. At first I treated that as a useful side task; later I realised it was the part of the week I looked forward to most. Two years ago I moved into corporate learning and now design training programmes for different departments. Oddly enough, I use my original degree every day, just not in the way I expected. That has taught me that changing direction doesn't always mean starting from zero.\n________________________________________",[]],["70e9a7ed-d21d-499e-b299-4373cf269a6a","a5f28363581007ad9f54d57258ebeda1","a290d2cd26605d5f621552670099c736","41c047c0ea1b26f6ce09768ff5b4de8b","574bf942a589dbe6d621dedca4789446","Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nWhen the Steering Wheel Lets Go\nFor years, images of driverless transport belonged to science fiction: silent vehicles gliding through cities while passengers read, sleep or work. Recently, however, that future has begun to look less distant. In several places, buses already follow fixed routes without a human driver, and robot taxis are being tested on public roads. Yet if you ask transport engineers when driverless travel will become normal, many give a careful answer. The technology has advanced quickly, they say, but the bigger challenge is fitting it into the untidy reality of everyday life.\nA machine can be trained to recognise traffic lights, lane markings and road signs with impressive accuracy. What causes trouble is not the ordinary journey but the unusual one: the child who runs into the street chasing a ball, the roadworks that force traffic into a temporary lane, the police officer waving drivers through a red light after an accident. Human drivers are not perfect in such situations, of course, but they are often good at guessing what others are about to do. Teaching that kind of judgement to software has proved far harder than early developers expected.\nBecause of this, some companies have moved away from grand promises. Instead of trying to build vehicles that can handle every road in every weather condition, they are concentrating on limited environments where fewer surprises appear. This is why airports, business parks and ports have become important testing grounds. A shuttle that drives slowly around a predictable route may not sound revolutionary, but supporters argue that such services matter. They can cut costs, reduce emissions and provide useful evidence about how people respond when no one is sitting behind the wheel.\nPublic reaction, in fact, may shape the future of the technology as much as computer design does. Surveys often show a contradiction: people like the idea of fewer crashes caused by tired or distracted drivers, but they also say they would feel nervous entering a vehicle controlled entirely by software. That hesitation is not simply fear of new technology. It also reflects a difficult question of responsibility. If a driverless bus makes a serious mistake, who should be blamed: the passenger, the operator, the manufacturer or the programmer who wrote the code? Until governments answer that clearly, widespread public trust will remain hard to win.\nThere is also the risk of expecting too much too soon. Some transport experts worry that the phrase driverless transport creates the false impression that humans will soon disappear from the system altogether. In reality, remote supervisors may still need to monitor vehicles, maintenance teams will have to check sensors constantly, and control-room staff may be required to step in when conditions become too complex. In that sense, automation may change human work rather than remove it. The jobs may look different, but people are unlikely to become irrelevant.\nEven so, the long-term effects could be significant. For older people or disabled passengers, autonomous services might offer greater independence, especially in areas with poor public transport. Freight companies are also interested in self-driving lorries that could travel long motorway stretches more efficiently. Still, the most sensible view is probably neither excitement nor disappointment. Driverless transport is unlikely to arrive all at once in a dramatic leap. More probably, it will appear bit by bit, in places where it solves specific problems better than existing systems do. The future may indeed be driverless, but only in parts, and only after a great deal of patient human effort.\n\nQuestions\n31. What point does the writer make in the opening paragraph about driverless transport? A. It is developing, but becoming part of daily life is more complicated than people first thought. B. It has already changed transport systems in most major cities more rapidly than expected. C. It is attracting engineers mainly because passengers want more time to relax during journeys. D. It will remain limited to science-fiction ideas unless governments invest far more money.\n32. In the second paragraph, the writer suggests that early developers misjudged A. how often traffic systems would need updating. B. how expensive it would be to train software engineers. C. how difficult it would be to prepare vehicles for unexpected events. D. how willing human drivers would be to share roads with machines.\n33. Why does the writer mention airports, business parks and ports in the third paragraph? A. to show where driverless transport has already replaced ordinary vehicles completely B. to illustrate the type of controlled settings that suit present-day experiments C. to criticise companies for avoiding places where transport problems are most serious D. to explain why passengers prefer short automated journeys to longer ones\n34. What is the writer's attitude to the public's concerns in the fourth paragraph? A. They are exaggerated because machines are already safer than people in most situations. B. They are disappointing because they slow progress that would clearly benefit everyone. C. They are understandable because important questions have not yet been settled. D. They are misplaced because passengers should accept responsibility for their own choices.\n35. In the fifth paragraph, what does ‘In that sense’ refer back to?\nA. Transport experts’ fear that the public will expect the technology to arrive too quickly.\nB. The view that new transport systems should be introduced first in controlled settings.\nC. The belief that members of the public will continue to distrust automated transport.\nD. The continuing need for people to monitor vehicles, maintain equipment and step in when needed.\n36. Which statement best summarises the writer's overall view in the final paragraph? A. Driverless freight services are likely to be more successful than public transport schemes. B. The benefits of autonomous transport are so great that delays should not discourage developers. C. Driverless transport will probably spread gradually where it offers practical advantages. D. The main future role of autonomous vehicles will be to support elderly and disabled travellers.",[["35 A) the belief that transport experts are expecting too much from the technology","35 A) Transport experts’ fear that the public will expect the technology to arrive too quickly.",false],["35 B) the possibility that vehicles will still need checking when conditions are difficult","35 B) The view that new transport systems should be introduced first in controlled settings.",false],["35 C) the claim that the term driverless transport creates confusion for the public","35 C) The belief that members of the public will continue to distrust automated transport.",false],["35 D) the idea that automation is more likely to alter jobs than remove people entirely","35 D) The continuing need for people to monitor vehicles, maintain equipment and step in when needed.",true]]],["a0f9a969-3c0e-4638-97dd-3434effc6db1","88be337e4e4f175168d8fa3cec941a51","4d8b3bc589bf979e1490a88b7611c1b9","094bf1a7c71b7575c3690b26c74a655c","0f650bfbaf4ede27375b03931c9aa642","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person…? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who began with a fairly limited reason for taking part, but later developed a deeper commitment?\n44 Who believes that educating others is now a more valuable use of time than the activity they were first known for?\n45 Who admits they were doubtful about expert advice until a relationship of trust was built?\n46 Who says dramatic stories attract donations even though they personally value preventing problems most?\n47 Who prefers steady, less visible progress to high-profile action that may not last?\n48 Who changed their working methods after recognising that their own behaviour might be part of the problem?\n49 Who mentions finding it difficult to persuade people until there is clear evidence in front of them?\n50 Who says restrictions were difficult for local people to accept while their earnings depended on the activity being restricted?\n51 Who feels that concern from inexperienced people should be treated sympathetically, even when it creates extra work?\n52 Who has become more willing to accept slow results, despite disappointment when resources are reduced?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Lena Morris\nInitially, I joined a local group that monitored otters simply because I enjoyed being outdoors. I had no plan to get involved in conservation in a serious way. However, after one winter survey, I realised how little people noticed what was happening along the riverbanks near our town. Since then, I’ve spent most weekends giving short talks to walkers and anglers, explaining why small disturbances can matter. What surprises me is that success often depends less on dramatic rescue stories and more on dull tasks like recording water quality and persuading landowners to move fencing. It can be frustratingly slow, yet I prefer that to quick campaigns that attract attention for a month and then disappear. If anything, I’ve become more patient, although I still find it hard when funding is cut just as a project begins to show results.\n________________________________________\nB – Tariq Shah\nFor years I photographed rare birds for travel magazines, and at first I saw endangered species mainly as subjects for striking images. Meanwhile, guides in several countries kept telling me the same thing: tourists wanted close views, but the pressure was changing the animals’ behaviour. That made me rethink my own role. Now I still take pictures, yet I work under much stricter rules and often put the camera down entirely. Oddly enough, the most useful thing I do is train younger photographers to recognise when they are too near a nest or feeding site. Some complain that the limits spoil the experience, but I remember making similar excuses myself. I’m not against tourism; in fact, local communities may depend on it. Even so, if visitors leave with only one lesson, I hope it is that admiration should never become interference.\n________________________________________\nC – Megan Doyle\nWhen our fishing business was asked to support a marine protection zone, my first reaction was suspicion. Like many others on the harbour, I assumed the scientists had arrived with tidy theories and no understanding of how families here earn a living. Yet one of them kept coming back, listening rather than lecturing, and gradually a few of us agreed to test seasonal no-fishing areas. The early months were difficult, especially when catches fell and neighbours said we had been naive. Since then, however, the evidence has become harder to ignore: some species are returning, and younger fish are appearing in places that had seemed empty. I still dislike being told what to do by people from outside the community, but I’ve accepted that protecting wildlife and protecting jobs are not always opposite aims. The challenge is convincing others before they see the proof for themselves.\n________________________________________\nD – Sofia Petrescu\nI run a small rescue centre for injured hedgehogs, so people assume I spend my time doing practical work with animals. In reality, a large part of the job is answering messages from well-meaning members of the public who have picked up healthy young ones by mistake. Although that can be exhausting, I try not to sound annoyed, because their concern comes from a good place. What troubles me more is the expectation that a centre like ours can solve the wider problem alone. We patch up the damage, certainly, but road design, garden fencing and pesticide use matter far more in the long term. Recently, I’ve started visiting schools, as children often persuade adults to change habits more effectively than leaflets do. It’s not glamorous, and donations usually increase only after a dramatic story appears online, yet prevention remains the part of the work I value most.\n________________________________________",[["50 B","50 B",false],["50 C","50 C",true]]],["6a6fdbd0-0bfb-4c85-bd18-b77e9ad212ce","45977029a5c9055251961fb108289074","525de267b2b488af0fe4fcd494776669","d67812f8e23c40cf9933e1486763d5ce","d67812f8e23c40cf9933e1486763d5ce","Read the article in which people talk about their experiences. For each question, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\nWhich person mentions the following? The people may be chosen more than once.\n43 Who came to appreciate an invention only after managing without it for a short period?\n44 Who accepts that a useful invention may also reduce a skill people once had?\n45 Who says that the information collected by an invention became more valuable to them than the time it saved at the checkout?\n46 Who chose an invention partly because it still works well without modern digital systems?\n47 Who changed their mind after discovering how much time and physical effort an old household task required?\n48 Who warns that one development linked to an invention can make human contact less pleasant?\n49 Who says an invention helps by freeing attention for another task?\n50 Who values an invention because it had a wider effect on how people organised their lives?\n51 Who started using an invention for one reason but continued because of an unexpected benefit?\n52 Who values an unobtrusive invention because it supports decisions customers rarely see, such as what a shop needs to order?\n________________________________________\nTexts\nA – Leila Khan\nWhen people ask which invention changed daily life most, I always say the washing machine. That sounds unexciting beside smartphones or electric cars, yet in my family it made the biggest difference. My grandmother grew up hand-washing everything, and she still talks about winter mornings with cracked hands and endless sheets. Initially, I thought she was exaggerating, but after our machine broke for a week, I understood. What struck me wasn't only the labour involved, but how much time disappeared. Since then, I've seen the appliance differently: not just as a convenience, but as something that quietly gave people, especially women, far more control over their day. However, I dislike how easily we take it for granted. We praise glamorous inventions, while this one transformed ordinary routines without demanding attention.\n________________________________________\nB – Oliver Grant\nFor me, the satnav has altered everyday life more than any other device. I work as a furniture maker and spend half my week driving to clients' homes, often in unfamiliar towns. Before I started using one, I relied on printed maps and scribbled directions, which meant arriving stressed or late. At first, I treated satnav instructions as absolute truth, and that was a mistake; once it led me down a farm track that certainly wasn't designed for a van. Even so, I wouldn't give it up. What matters is not that it makes journeys more exciting, but that it removes a layer of mental effort. Meanwhile, I can concentrate on traffic and timing instead of constantly checking road names. Some friends complain it has weakened people's sense of direction, and they may be right, yet I think the trade-off is worth it.\n________________________________________\nC – Rosa Mendez\nThe barcode seems too ordinary to deserve much attention, although I think it changed modern life in a remarkable way. I manage a small supermarket, so I see its effect every day. Customers notice the quick beep at the checkout, but they rarely think beyond that. Before our current system, stock checks took ages and mistakes were common. Now we can track what sells, spot problems quickly and order more accurately. Initially, I admired the speed alone; since then, I've come to value the information behind it even more. That said, I do feel uneasy when people assume technology always improves shopping. Self-service tills, for example, can make the experience colder. Still, the barcode itself solved practical problems without pretending to be clever. In my view, its success lies in being almost invisible while supporting an entire chain of everyday decisions.\n________________________________________\nD – Nathan Reed\nIf I had to choose one invention, I'd go for the bicycle. That may sound nostalgic in an age of apps and streaming, but I mean the ordinary bike as a tool for everyday freedom. I started cycling to work during a train strike, expecting it to be a temporary solution. Instead, it completely changed my routine. Initially, I noticed the obvious benefits: lower costs, no waiting for delayed services and a chance to avoid crowded carriages. Yet the real surprise was psychological. I arrived feeling more awake and less irritated, even in bad weather. Of course, cycling isn't practical for everyone, and poor road design puts many people off. However, I admire inventions that remain useful without requiring updates, passwords or subscriptions. The bicycle still does exactly what it was designed to do, and for many people that is enough.\n________________________________________",[]],["27e709f0-40db-4441-9fca-062e9b984bb8","10af17040ba56cad7dcb9da05ce590ec","81a38e0d8a53674dd426391a18670100","d7f86e51bba93885c7572fabca239e1d","93504c5ede229f15af8edfa79011026c","\nRead the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nLearning by Getting Stuck\nWhen the Riverside Museum announced that it was turning one gallery into a puzzle room for school groups, some teachers were doubtful. The museum was known for local history, not games, and several staff worried that pupils would spend the visit searching for codes instead of looking carefully at the objects. The first trial seemed to confirm those fears. Students rushed from case to case, shouting guesses and paying little attention to the information beside them. Yet the museum team did not abandon the idea. They changed the puzzles.\n\nThe original version rewarded speed. A number hidden in one display led directly to the next clue, so the quickest group could finish without understanding much. In the revised version, each task required students to compare two or three pieces of evidence. A shipping label had to be matched with a map; a photograph had to be dated using details in the background; a diary entry only made sense after pupils had identified who wrote it. The room became slower, and that was intentional.\n\nSomething else changed too. In ordinary museum visits, teachers often answer questions as soon as pupils ask them. During the puzzle sessions, staff were instructed to wait. If a group became stuck, the adult could offer a small hint, but not a solution. At first this felt uncomfortable. Teachers were used to helping efficiently, and some students became visibly frustrated. However, after a few minutes, many groups began dividing the problem between themselves. One pupil reread the clue, another checked the map, and a third noticed a detail everyone else had ignored.\n\nFor the museum's education officer, Amira Shah, that moment became the most interesting part of the project. She had expected the game to make history more entertaining. Instead, she found herself watching how students behaved when an answer was not immediately available. Some confident pupils discovered that guessing loudly was less useful than listening. Quieter students often contributed because the task gave them something concrete to point to. The historical content still mattered, but the session also became a lesson in how groups think.\n\nThis does not mean every subject should be turned into a puzzle. The museum's team has rejected requests to add points, timers and competitive leader boards. They found that these features encouraged students to focus on winning rather than evidence. Nor do they claim that a single visit teaches deep historical knowledge. What it can do, Shah argues, is create a reason to inspect information closely and tolerate a short period of uncertainty. Those habits are useful well beyond a museum.\n\nThe project has also changed how the museum designs ordinary displays. Curators now ask whether labels give visitors everything too quickly. A new exhibition sometimes places a question beside an object before providing the explanation several steps later. The aim is not to confuse people, but to let curiosity do some of the work. The museum has learned that difficulty is not always an obstacle to learning. In the right amount, and with the right support, it can be the thing that makes attention last.\n\nQuestions\n31. What concern did several museum staff express before the first trial?\nA. The gallery did not contain enough objects to make a puzzle.\nB. Pupils might hunt for answers without paying attention to the exhibits.\nC. Teachers might spend most of the visit explaining the rules.\nD. The activity might be too slow to fit into a school visit.\n\n32. What was the main weakness of the original version of the activity?\nA. It allowed students to progress without examining information deeply.\nB. It required teachers to explain too many historical details.\nC. It depended on information that was not available in the gallery.\nD. It took much longer than the museum had planned.\n\n33. What did teachers find difficult during the revised sessions?\nA. persuading confident students to take part\nB. deciding how to divide pupils into groups\nC. resisting the urge to provide answers immediately\nD. understanding the clues before the students did\n\n34. What surprised Amira Shah about the project?\nA. The most valuable learning was partly about group problem-solving.\nB. Quiet students knew more history than their teachers expected.\nC. Students preferred the puzzles to all other museum activities.\nD. Competitive pupils became less interested in completing the tasks.\n\n35. Why has the museum rejected points and leader boards?\nA. They are expensive to maintain for large school groups.\nB. They make it difficult to compare one class with another.\nC. They encourage attention to performance rather than evidence.\nD. They work only when students already know the subject well.\n\n36. What broader lesson has the museum taken from the project?\nA. Visitors learn best when displays provide as little information as possible.\nB. A carefully controlled amount of difficulty can strengthen attention.\nC. Historical exhibitions should be redesigned mainly for schoolchildren.\nD. Curiosity is more important than accurate information in museum learning.\n",[["31 A) They thought the museum lacked enough objects to create a game.","31 A) The gallery did not contain enough objects to make a puzzle.",false],["31 B) They feared the game would distract pupils from the historical material.","31 B) Pupils might hunt for answers without paying attention to the exhibits.",true],["31 C) They believed school groups would refuse to work together.","31 C) Teachers might spend most of the visit explaining the rules.",false],["31 D) They expected the puzzles to be too difficult for most pupils.","31 D) The activity might be too slow to fit into a school visit.",false]]],["10775346-8627-4523-9797-8ac032cf7da4","5e92b12079f441ee26fb861e10797cb9","6caff307f81adf7888c2e92ab8505c47","a62f88b6dd4e52e326f23e532cf5528d","a62f88b6dd4e52e326f23e532cf5528d","\nRead the article in which four people describe a food-related experience while travelling. For questions 43–52, choose from the people (A–D). The people may be chosen more than once.\n\nWhich person…?\n43 discovered that the most useful part of a visit happened before most tourists arrived?\n44 admits that they had misunderstood the amount of skill involved in an everyday product?\n45 says they were given a task to do while other people continued working?\n46 says that conversation became easier because everyone had a practical job to do?\n47 became less interested in finding the “best” place after the experience?\n48 was surprised that environmental conditions affected the food more immediately than expected?\n49 mentions learning why a local product cannot simply be made the same way everywhere?\n50 appreciated an experience that changed according to what had happened that day?\n51 realised that a meal could reveal family relationships as well as recipes?\n52 now pays more attention to the way people share meals when visiting another place?\n\nA – Maya, food-tour participant\nI had booked a food tour, but the guide emailed the night before to ask whether I would mind meeting at 5.30 a.m. instead. I nearly said no. That early start turned out to be the entire point. By seven, when most tourists were arriving, the serious buying was almost finished. I watched restaurant owners inspect boxes, argue cheerfully about prices and change their menus according to what looked good that morning. Nobody performed for us; we simply stayed out of the way. The experience stopped me asking, “Which restaurant is best?” I now ask where ingredients come from and what happens before they reach a plate.\n\nB – Daniel, neighbourhood bakery class\nBread seemed simple to me: flour, water, yeast, heat. During a morning in a family bakery, I discovered how wrong that was. The baker kept changing tiny things because the air was unusually humid. Dough that had behaved perfectly the day before needed more time and less water. I had assumed good bread came from following an exact recipe, but much of the skill was judgement built through repetition. The baker also explained why a loaf made with a particular local flour would never taste exactly the same somewhere else. I left respecting an ordinary object far more than before.\n\nC – Priya, fishing co-operative visit\nI expected a demonstration. Instead, the co-operative manager handed me a waterproof apron and asked me to help sort labelled boxes while people worked around us. That made conversation much easier because nobody had to sit politely making small talk. The interesting part was that nothing was fixed. If the boats returned late, the whole schedule moved. If one species was scarce, restaurants changed what they ordered. I liked the fact that the visit reflected a real working day rather than a version prepared for visitors.\n\nD – Owen, family Sunday lunch\nA friend invited me to his aunt's house and warned me that lunch might last four hours. He was right. I learned some recipes, but what stayed with me was how the meal organised the family. One cousin always served the first course, an uncle cut the bread, and the grandmother decided when everyone could move on to dessert. There was plenty of teasing, and old disagreements surfaced without ruining the atmosphere. I realised that food can show who looks after whom, who has authority and who is allowed to break the rules. Since then, when I travel, I pay more attention to how people eat together, not only to what is on the table.\n",[]],["6dbc0539-2b9f-452d-89dd-96af1de5677f","b9e64d8256fccfc2362e9d294b52d79e","250931eeca79f21d39af810d7a08ff0f","b26744aba7d63bb3035882dad7d7cbd6","b26744aba7d63bb3035882dad7d7cbd6","\nSix sentences have been removed from the article. Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There is one extra sentence which you do not need to use.\nText\nWhen a Prototype Refuses to Die\nEngineers often describe prototypes as experiments rather than products. Their purpose is to reveal what works, what fails and what nobody had thought about. (37) Yet teams can become so focused on the original goal that they overlook a useful idea hiding inside an unsuccessful design.\n\nA small product company discovered this while developing a portable desk fan. The first model was quiet and powerful, but its folding stand was unreliable. After several weeks, the team abandoned the design. (38) One engineer kept using the flexible joint from the stand to hold a light above his workbench.\n\nThat casual use led to a new question. The joint had failed because it was expected to support the weight of the entire fan, but it worked perfectly for a much lighter object. (39) The team began testing it in reading lights, phone holders and small display stands, where the original weakness was no longer a problem.\n\nThis kind of second use is easier to find when teams record why something failed. A simple note such as “not strong enough” is less useful than a description of the exact conditions in which the part stopped working. (40) Detailed failure records can therefore become a source of ideas rather than a list of disappointments.\n\nThe attitude also changes how people work together. If every failed prototype is treated as evidence that someone made a bad decision, staff become cautious and hide problems. (41) Designers are then more likely to test unusual options early, when changing direction is still cheap.\n\nOf course, not every mistake contains a new product. Most failed ideas are simply lessons about what not to do. (42) The important point is to separate the value of the experiment from the success of the first plan. A prototype can fail at one job and still teach a team enough to succeed somewhere else.\n\nSentences\nA That information helps designers recognise whether the same limitation would matter in a different use.\nB Even so, looking again costs little compared with starting every idea from nothing.\nC The rejected model was boxed up, but one part never made it into storage.\nD In many cases, that means an early model is expected to be thrown away.\nE Once the load was reduced, the part's flexibility became an advantage rather than a weakness.\nF A healthier culture treats early failure as information that belongs to the whole team.\nG Some companies now sell replacement fans in more than twenty colours.\n",[]],["d43c9f02-ecbf-488b-a414-01ba37024545","6c588040f53f19d98d0209f58f7ed196","6b6f8c8fdf02138cdc5bf01f0b911be8","c5218ccee695179e402f00e87fb12eb4","c5218ccee695179e402f00e87fb12eb4","\nRead the article in which four people describe community music projects. For questions 43–52, choose from the people (A–D).\n\n43 Who says people started returning more regularly once the group dropped a planned performance?\n44 Who values a project because it helps different age groups exchange knowledge in both directions?\n45 Who mentions changing the location of an activity in order to reach people who would not attend a formal venue?\n46 Who describes a committee choosing the songs before the group had had a chance to decide what it wanted to sing?\n47 Who says a practical shared task makes conversation easier between people of different ages?\n48 Who believes the project matters partly because local people can hear familiar places differently?\n49 Who noticed that younger participants contributed technical skills that older participants lacked?\n50 Who says success is measured by regular attendance rather than musical quality?\n51 Who explains that unexpected sounds from the environment became part of the activity?\n52 Who feels that participants should have more control over what is performed?\n\nA – Harpreet, neighbourhood choir organiser\nOur choir began with a list of songs chosen by the committee and a concert date already booked. It looked organised, but people were nervous and kept apologising for their voices. We eventually cancelled the concert and spent a term letting members choose what they wanted to sing. Attendance improved immediately. Some people still enjoy performing, but I now judge the project by whether they return each week. The biggest lesson was that we had treated performance as the purpose when, for many members, singing together was enough.\n\nB – Lewis, intergenerational recording project\nWe pair teenagers with older residents to record songs connected with the area. The older participants know melodies, stories and sometimes dialect words the students have never heard. The teenagers, meanwhile, know how to edit sound, remove background noise and share recordings online. I like that the learning goes both ways. Conversation can be awkward at first, so we give each pair a practical job: choose one verse, record it and decide what explanation is needed. Once they are working on something, the age gap matters less.\n\nC – Nia, street-music workshop leader\nWe used to run workshops in a civic arts centre, but the same confident musicians came every month. So we moved several sessions to markets, basketball courts and a covered walkway near the station. That changed who stopped to join in. We also stopped fighting the environment. Trains, vendors and traffic appear in the recordings, and sometimes we build rhythms around them. I love the idea that people can hear a place they use every day as if it were part of a composition.\n\nD – Tomas, community band coordinator\nOur band includes beginners and experienced players, so choosing music can be political. At first, the tutors selected pieces they believed were “suitable”. The result was technically sensible and emotionally flat. We now ask members to nominate music connected with weddings, football matches, religious celebrations or family parties, then arrange it for the instruments we have. The players care more because the repertoire belongs to them. I still offer guidance, but I no longer think professional judgement should automatically have the final word.\n",[]],["a3f98493-251a-4718-9413-787b714ff893","314c41b2c3a3fdb3486c8f9916a899a5","7b355f97b0669c471bcea27ae415f00d","378eacd787af8dc8e5b943b7df6b55be","b10f4b3d343b8013eac711b658a1b6a2","\nRead the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to the text.\nText\nListening to a Landscape\nWhen people imagine exploring the ocean, they usually picture cameras, bright lights and a vehicle moving through dark water. Marine researcher Helen Moore uses all of those tools, but she also spends long hours listening. Her team places underwater microphones in coastal areas and leaves them there for weeks. The recordings are not designed to produce beautiful nature programmes. They are a way of observing a place when nobody is physically present.\n\nSound travels efficiently through water, which means a microphone can detect activity beyond the distance a camera can see. A recording may contain boat engines, waves, snapping shrimp and the calls of marine animals. None of those sounds tells a complete story on its own. The challenge is to identify patterns: what changes between day and night, what appears after a storm, or what disappears when boat traffic increases.\n\nAt first, Moore expected the main difficulty to be recognising individual sounds. That is certainly part of the work, but the larger problem is context. The same sound can mean different things in different places. A loud engine may be unusual in a protected bay but completely normal near a harbour. A quiet period may indicate calm conditions, or simply that a microphone has been partly covered by sand. For this reason, acoustic data is usually combined with weather records, maps and direct observations.\n\nThe method is particularly useful because human visits can alter the environment being studied. Divers create bubbles and movement; boats add noise; lights change what animals can see. A microphone left in place is not invisible, but after installation it can collect information with relatively little disturbance. It can also record moments researchers would otherwise miss, including night-time activity and sudden changes during bad weather.\n\nHowever, the amount of data creates its own problem. A single project can produce thousands of hours of recordings. Software can help find repeated patterns, but Moore warns against assuming that automated detection is always correct. Background noise can confuse systems, and unusual events are often the very things an algorithm has seen least often. Researchers therefore use computers to narrow the material, then return to the recordings themselves.\n\nThe most interesting result, Moore says, is often not a rare animal call but a change in the whole soundscape. A coastline may become quieter after a new speed limit for boats, or certain natural sounds may return during a seasonal closure. These shifts do not prove cause and effect on their own, but they tell researchers where to look more closely. Listening is therefore not a replacement for other forms of exploration. It is a way of deciding which questions deserve the next trip.\n\nQuestions\n31. What is the main purpose of the underwater microphones in the first paragraph?\nA. to create recordings for public nature programmes\nB. to observe places for long periods without researchers being there\nC. to replace cameras in areas where visibility is poor\nD. to identify individual animals more accurately than divers can\n\n32. What is the writer illustrating with the examples of day and night, storms and boat traffic?\nA. Recordings are most useful if researchers can predict when the sea will be quiet.\nB. Some sounds are useful only when researchers can identify the species making them.\nC. Changes in what is heard can reveal patterns that individual sounds cannot show.\nD. Researchers should record storms separately from periods of human activity.\n\n33. What does the example of an engine in a bay and near a harbour illustrate?\nA. The same sound can carry different significance in different places.\nB. A loud engine is easiest to identify when there are few other sounds.\nC. Sound levels normally tell researchers how far away a boat is.\nD. A harbour is usually the best place to collect data about boats.\n\n34. What advantage of the method is described in paragraph four?\nA. It allows observation with less disturbance after equipment is installed.\nB. It provides clearer information than direct observation in every situation.\nC. It can identify the exact location of animals during storms.\nD. It requires less preparation than using divers or research boats.\n\n35. Why does Moore remain cautious about automated analysis?\nA. It works only when recordings contain very little background noise.\nB. It may be weakest at recognising unusual events.\nC. It produces too few results for researchers to compare.\nD. It cannot process recordings made at night.\n\n36. What does the final paragraph suggest about changes in a soundscape?\nA. They can guide researchers towards questions worth investigating further.\nB. They usually prove that a conservation measure has succeeded.\nC. They are useful only when a rare species can be heard clearly.\nD. They make direct fieldwork unnecessary in well-monitored areas.\n",[["32 A) Marine sounds are too quiet to identify reliably.","32 A) Recordings are most useful if researchers can predict when the sea will be quiet.",false],["32 B) Most recordings contain several species at the same time.","32 B) Some sounds are useful only when researchers can identify the species making them.",false],["32 C) Researchers need patterns and context in order to interpret them.","32 C) Changes in what is heard can reveal patterns that individual sounds cannot show.",true],["32 D) The microphones often fail to record human activity.","32 D) Researchers should record storms separately from periods of human activity.",false],["33 A) The meaning of a sound depends heavily on where and when it occurs.","33 A) The same sound can carry different significance in different places.",true],["33 B) Weather records are less reliable than she had expected.","33 B) A loud engine is easiest to identify when there are few other sounds.",false],["33 C) Harbours are quieter than protected coastal areas.","33 C) Sound levels normally tell researchers how far away a boat is.",false],["33 D) Microphones are more easily damaged by sand than by storms.","33 D) A harbour is usually the best place to collect data about boats.",false]]],["63d67a2e-1bd1-484a-b96c-c75ca5df24f1","21d39047ecc78657c9a2e04595134aa3","0e3abba1ef722197b3127a864833fd7f","90ba723d97631d93e2cd5790f83a4b41","90ba723d97631d93e2cd5790f83a4b41","\nRead the article in which four people describe changes to their shopping habits. For questions 43–52, choose from A–D.\n\n43 Who introduced a delay between wanting something and deciding whether to buy it?\n44 Who discovered that access to an item could matter more than owning it?\n45 Who says a strict personal rule was easier to follow than making repeated decisions?\n46 Who realised that browsing itself, rather than genuine need, was creating purchases?\n47 Who mentions that another person's opinion helps reveal whether an item is genuinely useful?\n48 Who still enjoys shopping but has changed the stage at which money is spent?\n49 Who joined a borrowing scheme to save space and ended up getting to know neighbours?\n50 Who admits that their approach would not work equally well for everyone?\n51 Who says removing a source of advertising reduced the number of desires they had to resist?\n52 Who prefers borrowing because storage space is limited?\n\nA – Leila\nI use a 72-hour list. If I see something I want online, I write it down with the price but do nothing else for three days. Most items look less urgent by then. I still enjoy browsing, and I have not stopped buying clothes or books, but the payment happens later than the excitement. I also send the list to my sister sometimes. She is very good at asking the annoying question: “Where exactly will you use that?” I do not always listen, but having to answer usually tells me whether the purchase makes sense.\n\nB – Marcus\nMy problem was not expensive objects. It was small purchases made constantly because shopping apps were always in front of me. I removed them from my phone and unsubscribed from promotional emails. The surprising part was that I did not spend the next month heroically resisting temptation. I simply thought about fewer products. I still buy things online when I need them, but I search deliberately instead of wandering through recommendations. For me, changing what I saw was easier than trying to become more disciplined.\n\nC – Priya\nI live in a small flat and do a lot of DIY, which is a bad combination if you own every tool you might use twice a year. I joined a local “library of things” where members borrow drills, carpet cleaners and camping equipment. I originally joined to save space, but I now value the people too. Members swap advice, and I have met neighbours I had never spoken to. Borrowing is not perfect — popular items need booking — but for occasional equipment I would rather have reliable access than permanent ownership.\n\nD – Tom\nFor one year I decided that almost everything I bought, except food and safety items, had to be second-hand. The rule sounded extreme, yet it was easier than asking myself hundreds of times whether a new product was justified. I still bought plenty, just from charity shops, repair centres and resale sites. The limit forced me to wait because the exact item was not always available immediately. I would not recommend the same rule to everyone; it worked because I enjoy searching. But it changed my sense of what “normal” shopping looks like.\n",[]],["90731247-81da-4dca-a610-8c2504bae125","ddf189d034f876f42cae5e0ae7c54896","630ec23609c3a87762f86452336173c3","bf6af8052826a17a11cc91aa2cd43262","bf6af8052826a17a11cc91aa2cd43262","\nRead the article in which four adults describe learning a language through a practical activity. For questions 43–52, choose from A–D.\n\n43 Who found that a repeated practical phrase became useful before they fully understood the grammar?\n44 Who could keep a conversation going after using the wrong word by pointing to the bicycle part they were working on?\n45 Who realised that listening skills improved before speaking became more confident?\n46 Who values learning expressions connected with a local tradition?\n47 Who found that responsibility to other people created stronger motivation than private study?\n48 Who says physical demonstration helped when vocabulary was missing?\n49 Who mentions that the activity exposed them to different ages and speaking styles?\n50 Who became less worried about speaking perfectly after seeing that communication could still succeed?\n51 Who says the activity gave them a reason to prepare language in advance?\n52 Who learnt repeated instructions about adjusting equipment by hearing them while people worked on the same kind of problem?\n\nA – Marta, community garden volunteer\nI joined a neighbourhood garden because I wanted to meet people, not because I had a language plan. The first phrase I learned properly was basically “hold this here”, because somebody said it to me every Saturday while we tied plants to supports. I copied it long before I understood why the words were in that order. Gardening helped because people could point, demonstrate and repeat. If I did not know the word for a tool, someone simply showed me. After a few months I noticed that the same practical language had become automatic.\n\nB – Owen, amateur football referee\nStudying alone was easy to cancel. Refereeing local matches was different because other people depended on me. I had to learn how to give clear instructions before kick-off and explain decisions quickly. I started writing useful phrases on cards the night before a match. The pressure was real, but it was focused: nobody cared about perfect grammar if the message was clear and fair. That experience made me much less afraid of speaking.\n\nC – Priya, local choir member\nFor the first few weeks I hardly spoke. I listened to the conductor, watched other singers and tried to understand jokes during breaks. The strange thing was that my listening improved long before I felt brave enough to join conversations. The choir includes teenagers, parents and retired people, so I hear very different voices and speeds. I also learned expressions connected with local songs and celebrations that never appeared in my textbook. That cultural connection is now one of my favourite parts.\n\nD – Daniel, bicycle-repair co-operative\nAt the workshop, conversation happens while everybody is looking at the same problem. That removed a lot of pressure. If I used the wrong word, I could point at the brake cable and continue. I began to notice repeated phrases such as “try it again”, “a bit tighter” and “does that feel right?” Hearing them every week in nearly identical situations made them stick. The workshop did not make me fluent suddenly, but it taught me that successful communication can happen while language is still incomplete.\n",[]]]$payload$::jsonb)
  LOOP
    question_id := (item->>0)::uuid;
    SELECT md5(q.enunciado) INTO STRICT stem_hash
    FROM public.levels_preguntas q WHERE q.id = question_id FOR UPDATE;
    PERFORM 1 FROM public.levels_respuestas r WHERE r.pregunta_id = question_id FOR UPDATE;
    SELECT md5(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_array(r.respuesta,r.correcta) ORDER BY r.respuesta)::text)
      INTO answer_hash FROM public.levels_respuestas r WHERE r.pregunta_id = question_id;
    IF stem_hash = item->>2 AND answer_hash = item->>4 THEN
      CONTINUE;
    END IF;
    IF stem_hash IS DISTINCT FROM item->>1 OR answer_hash IS DISTINCT FROM item->>3 THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'Reviewed B2 content changed for %', question_id;
    END IF;

    IF answer_hash IS DISTINCT FROM item->>4 THEN
      FOR answer IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(item->6)
      LOOP
        UPDATE public.levels_respuestas r
        SET respuesta = answer->>1, correcta = (answer->>2)::boolean
        WHERE r.pregunta_id = question_id AND r.respuesta = answer->>0;
        GET DIAGNOSTICS changed = ROW_COUNT;
        IF changed <> 1 THEN
          RAISE EXCEPTION 'Expected one existing answer for %', question_id;
        END IF;
      END LOOP;
    END IF;
    SELECT md5(jsonb_agg(jsonb_build_array(r.respuesta,r.correcta) ORDER BY r.respuesta)::text)
      INTO answer_hash FROM public.levels_respuestas r WHERE r.pregunta_id = question_id;
    IF answer_hash IS DISTINCT FROM item->>4 THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected final answer key for %', question_id;
    END IF;

    UPDATE public.levels_preguntas q SET enunciado = item->>5 WHERE q.id = question_id;
  END LOOP;
END;
$release$;
